(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["yogatattva"]={"slug":"yogatattva","name":"Yogatattva","deva":"योगतत्त्व","note":"selected verses","intro":["The Complete Text<br>All 142 Verses · Devanāgarī · IAST · English Translation<br>With Word-by-Word for Key Verses &amp; Visual Analysis<br><br>Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda · 41st in the Muktikā 108<br>#16 among the Yoga Upaniṣads","The Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad (योगतत्त्वोपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad on the Principles of Yoga\") is one of the most practically detailed of the Yoga Upaniṣads. It belongs to the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda tradition (in its long recension) and is enumerated as the 41st Upaniṣad in the Muktikā's list of 108."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad (योगतत्त्वोपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad on the Principles of Yoga\") is one of the most practically detailed of the Yoga Upaniṣads. It belongs to the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda tradition (in its long recension) and is enumerated as the 41st Upaniṣad in the Muktikā's list of 108.","The text is presented as a dialogue: Brahmā (Pitāmaha, the Lord of Creatures) approaches Viṣṇu (Hṛṣīkeśa) and asks: \"Tell me the yoga-tattva together with its eight limbs (aṣṭāṅga-saṃyutam).\" What follows is Viṣṇu's comprehensive instruction — covering the philosophical premises, the four kinds of yoga (mantra/laya/haṭha/rāja), the four stages of progress, the eight limbs in detail, the seven mahā-mudrās, the five-element concentrations (pañca-dhāraṇā), the signs of accomplishment, the eight siddhis, and the culmination in samādhi and jīvanmukti.","It is, in effect, a compact and complete manual of classical yoga — predating the Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā by centuries and serving as a major source for that and later texts."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The Two Recensions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Modern scholarship recognizes that the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad exists in two substantially different versions:","Short recension (Atharva Veda) — only 15 verses. Brief and primarily philosophical.","Long recension (Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda) — 142 verses. Comprehensive yoga manual. THIS is the version covered in this document.","The long recension is the one quoted and translated by the major traditional and modern commentators (K. Narayanasvami Aiyar, T.R. Srinivasa Ayyangar, A.G. Krishna Warrier). When teachers refer to \"the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad,\" they almost always mean this 142-verse text."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Place in the Yoga Upaniṣad Canon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Among the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads (see the Vedas Complete Reference for the full list), the Yoga Tattva holds a foundational place:","Yoga Tattva — the structural framework (this text)","Śāṇḍilya — comprehensive 8-limb treatment with similar concerns","Yoga-Kuṇḍalinī — focuses specifically on kuṇḍalinī mechanics","Yoga-Śikhā — the longest and most systematic of all Yoga Upaniṣads","Dhyāna-Bindu, Nāda-Bindu, Tejo-Bindu — the subtle bindu-yogas","The Yoga Tattva is the natural starting point. It introduces the vocabulary, the structure, and the practical sequence that other Yoga Upaniṣads then develop in specialized directions."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. Structural Overview","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Structural overview of the 142 verses of the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The Four Yogas (vv. 19, 21-29)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The four yogas as taught in the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad","Verse 19 establishes the fourfold classification:","yogo hi bahudhā brahman bhidyate vyavahārataḥ /<br>mantrayogo layaścaiva haṭho 'sau rājayogataḥ //19//","\"Yoga, O Brahmā, is divided manifoldly by practice: Mantra-yoga, Laya-yoga, Haṭha-yoga, and Rāja-yoga.\"","Note the hierarchy implied: each yoga is \"higher\" than the previous, with Rāja-yoga as the crown. The text describes mantra-yoga briefly (vv. 21-22), laya-yoga briefly (v. 23), and haṭha-yoga in extensive detail (vv. 24-128). Rāja-yoga is described as the natural fruit of all the prior practices (vv. 129-130)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The Four Stages of Yoga (v. 20)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The four stages every yogi passes through","Verse 20 specifies the four states (avasthā) of yogic progress:","ārambhaśca ghaṭaścaiva tathā paricayaḥ smṛtaḥ /<br>niṣpattiścetyavasthā ca sarvatra parikīrtitā //20//","\"Ārambha (beginning), Ghaṭa (vessel), Paricaya (acquaintance), and Niṣpatti (completion) — these states are universally proclaimed.\"","<table><tr><td>Stage</td><td>Sanskrit</td><td>Description</td><td>Verses</td></tr><tr><td>1. Ārambha</td><td>आरम्भ</td><td>Nāḍī-śuddhi achieved after 3 months; lightness, brightness, digestion increased</td><td>vv. 30-64</td></tr><tr><td>2. Ghaṭa</td><td>घट</td><td>Prāṇa, apāna, mind, intellect, jīvātmā, paramātmā come into unity</td><td>vv. 65-79</td></tr><tr><td>3. Paricaya</td><td>परिचय</td><td>Kuṇḍalī awakened; mind enters suṣumnā; element-dhāraṇās</td><td>vv. 80-103</td></tr><tr><td>4. Niṣpatti</td><td>निष्पत्ति</td><td>Samādhi; equality of jīvātmā and paramātmā; jīvanmukti</td><td>vv. 104-130</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The Eight Limbs (vv. 24-25, 28-29)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The eight limbs as taught in the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad","Verses 24-25 list the classical aṣṭāṅga (eight-limbed) yoga:","yamaśca niyamaścaiva āsanaṃ prāṇasaṃyamaḥ /<br>pratyāhāro dhāraṇā ca dhyānaṃ bhrūmadhyame harim //<br>samādhiḥ samatāvasthā sāṣṭāṅgo yoga ucyate //24-25//","This is identical to Patañjali's aṣṭāṅga (Yoga Sūtras 2.29). What is striking is the Yoga Tattva's simplification: while Patañjali enumerates five yamas and five niyamas, the Yoga Tattva (vv. 28-29) makes a remarkable move:","\"laghv-āhāro yameṣv eko mukhyā bhavati netaraḥ /<br>ahiṃsā niyameṣv ekā mukhyā vai caturānana\" //28-29ab//","\"Among the yamas, laghu-āhāra (taking little food) alone is chief — not the others. Among the niyamas, ahiṃsā (non-violence) alone is chief, O Four-faced one.\"","A striking reduction: the moral discipline is condensed to TWO essentials — eat lightly, harm no being. This is the Yoga Tattva's practical-minded character.","Of the āsanas (v. 29), only four are listed:","Siddha — the perfect posture","Padma — the lotus posture","Siṃha — the lion posture","Bhadra — the auspicious posture","All later Haṭha-Yoga texts (Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā, Gheraṇḍa-Saṃhitā) expand this list, but YT keeps it compact."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The Prāṇāyāma Protocol (vv. 36-44)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The classical 16:64:32 prāṇāyāma protocol","The Yoga Tattva's prāṇāyāma instruction is among the clearest in any Yoga Upaniṣad. The protocol:","Sit in Padmāsana in a clean, draft-free, secluded hut (vv. 32-35)","Right thumb closes the right nostril (piṅgalā)","Pūraka (inhale) through left (iḍā) — 16 mātrās (counts)","Kumbhaka (retain) with both closed — 64 mātrās","Recaka (exhale) through right (piṅgalā) — 32 mātrās","Then reverse: inhale right, retain, exhale left","Do four sessions daily: dawn, noon, dusk, midnight (v. 43)","Build to 80 cycles per session, gradually (v. 43)","A mātrā is the time to snap fingers after going around the knee (v. 40) — roughly one second. The 1:4:2 ratio (inhale:retain:exhale) is the classical formula that all subsequent Haṭha-yoga texts inherit from this Upaniṣad.","After three months: nāḍī-śuddhi — the eight signs of purification (vv. 44-45):","Lightness of body (śarīra-laghutā)","Brightness / glow (dīpti)","Thinning of body (kṛśatva)","Reduced urine, excrement, sleep","Cessation of eye-discharge, saliva, sweat, bad odor"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"9. The Eight Siddhis (vv. 73-75)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The eight siddhis that arise during practice — and the warning against them","At the ghaṭa stage, the yogi develops \"siddhis\" — yogic powers. The Yoga Tattva lists eight (vv. 73-75):","<table><tr><td>Siddhi</td><td>Sanskrit</td><td>Power</td></tr><tr><td>Dūra-śruti</td><td>दूरश्रुति</td><td>Distant hearing</td></tr><tr><td>Dūra-dṛṣṭi</td><td>दूरदृष्टि</td><td>Distant vision</td></tr><tr><td>Dūra-gama</td><td>दूरगम</td><td>Distant motion (in an instant)</td></tr><tr><td>Vāk-siddhi</td><td>वाक्सिद्धि</td><td>Speech-perfection</td></tr><tr><td>Kāmarūpatva</td><td>कामरूपत्व</td><td>Form-at-will</td></tr><tr><td>Adṛśya-karaṇī</td><td>अदृश्यकरणी</td><td>Invisibility</td></tr><tr><td>Loha-svarṇatā</td><td>लोहसुवर्णता</td><td>Transmutation (iron → gold)</td></tr><tr><td>Khe-gati</td><td>खेगति</td><td>Sky-walking</td></tr></table>","Verse 76 carries the famous warning:","\"ete vighnā mahā-siddher na rametteṣu buddhimān /<br>na darśayet svasāmarthyaṃ yasya-kasyāpi yogirāṭ\" //76//","\"These (powers) are obstacles to the great accomplishment; the wise one should not delight in them. The chief among yogis should not show his powers to anyone whatsoever.\"","The siddhis are real but secondary. The seeker of liberation must pass through them without being captured."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"10. The Pañca-Dhāraṇā (vv. 83-103)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The five-element concentrations — each held for ~2 hours","The Pañcadhāraṇā — five concentrations on the five elements — is the most distinctive practice-system of the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad. Each element has its bodily region, geometric shape, color, bīja-mantra, and presiding deity. The yogi concentrates on each for \"five ghaṭikās\" (= 5 × 24 minutes = 2 hours):","<table><tr><td>Element</td><td>Region</td><td>Shape</td><td>Color</td><td>Bīja</td><td>Deity</td></tr><tr><td>Pṛthivī (Earth)</td><td>Feet → Knees</td><td>Square</td><td>Yellow</td><td>LAṂ (लं)</td><td>Brahmā (4-faced, gold)</td></tr><tr><td>Āpas (Water)</td><td>Knees → Anus</td><td>Half-moon</td><td>White</td><td>VAṂ (वं)</td><td>Nārāyaṇa (crystal, yellow-clad)</td></tr><tr><td>Agni (Fire)</td><td>Anus → Heart</td><td>Triangle</td><td>Red</td><td>RAṂ (रं)</td><td>Rudra (3-eyed, ash-smeared)</td></tr><tr><td>Vāyu (Air)</td><td>Heart → Eyebrows</td><td>Six-sided</td><td>Black</td><td>YAṂ (यं)</td><td>Īśvara (omniscient, all-faced)</td></tr><tr><td>Ākāśa (Ether)</td><td>Eyebrows → Crown</td><td>Circle</td><td>Smoky</td><td>HAṂ (हं)</td><td>Sadāśiva (5-faced, 10-armed)</td></tr></table>","Each dhāraṇā grants conquest of that element: by earth-dhāraṇā, no death by earth; by water-dhāraṇā, no fear of water; by fire-dhāraṇā, not burnt by fire; by air-dhāraṇā, sky-movement; by ākāśa-dhāraṇā, khecaratva (sky-walking) and supreme bliss.","After all five are mastered, the body becomes \"firm\" — no longer subject to death even at the cosmic dissolution (v. 103). This is the Yoga Tattva's unique contribution to the Vedic yoga literature — it predates and informs the Haṭha-Yoga texts."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"11. The Seven Mahā-Mudrās (vv. 26-27, 112-128)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The seven mudrās and bandhas of the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad","Beyond the eight aṣṭāṅga limbs, the Yoga Tattva adds a distinctive collection of physical-energetic practices — mudrās (\"seals\") and bandhas (\"locks\"):","<table><tr><td>Mudrā / Bandha</td><td>Sanskrit</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>Mahāmudrā</td><td>महामुद्रा</td><td>Left heel at perineum + right leg extended + chin to heart + kumbhaka</td></tr><tr><td>Mahābandha</td><td>महाबन्ध</td><td>Same as Mahāmudrā but with extended leg placed on thigh</td></tr><tr><td>Mahāvedha</td><td>महावेध</td><td>From Mahābandha, with breath restrained and ears sealed</td></tr><tr><td>Khecarī</td><td>खेचरी</td><td>Tongue turned back into the cranial cavity; gaze between brows</td></tr><tr><td>Jālandhara-bandha</td><td>जालन्धरबन्ध</td><td>Throat contracted, chin pressed to heart — \"the lion against death-elephant\"</td></tr><tr><td>Uḍḍiyāna-bandha</td><td>उड्डियानबन्ध</td><td>The bandha by which prāṇa rises through suṣumnā</td></tr><tr><td>Mūla-bandha</td><td>मूलबन्ध</td><td>Perineum pressed, apāna drawn upward</td></tr><tr><td>Vajrolī (etc.)</td><td>वज्रोली</td><td>Plus amarolī, sahajolī — the three \"bindu-mastery\" practices</td></tr></table>","These practices later become the staples of Haṭha Yoga. The Yoga Tattva is one of the earliest texts to systematically describe them. The Khecarī-mudrā in particular has a long subsequent history; the Yoga Tattva's description (v. 117) is one of the earliest extant."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Key Verses with Word-by-Word","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Twelve foundational verses of the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad with complete grammatical analysis. These are the verses every student of this text must understand at a precise word-level."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"The opening declaration","sa":["योगतत्त्वं प्रवक्ष्यामि योगिनां हितकाम्यया ।","यच्छृत्वा च पठित्वा च सर्वपापैः प्रमुच्यते ॥"],"iast":["yogatattvaṃ pravakṣyāmi yogināṃ hita-kāmyayā |","yacchṛtvā ca paṭhitvā ca sarva-pāpaiḥ pramucyate ||"],"words":[["yoga-tattvam","n. acc. sg.","the principle / essence of yoga"],["pravakṣyāmi","1st sg. fut.","I shall declare"],["yoginām","m. gen. pl.","of yogis / for yogis"],["hita-kāmyayā","f. instr. sg.","with desire for [their] welfare"],["yat","rel. n. acc. sg.","which"],["śrutvā","gerund of √śru","having heard"],["ca","particle","and"],["paṭhitvā","gerund of √paṭh","having recited"],["sarva-pāpaiḥ","n. instr. pl.","from all sins"],["pramucyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is liberated / freed"]],"lit":"I shall declare the principle of yoga for the welfare of yogis — by hearing and reciting which, one is freed from all sins.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The text's self-declaration. Note \"śrutvā ca paṭhitvā ca\" — hearing AND reciting. The Upaniṣad is meant to be both heard from a teacher and recited by the practitioner. This is a śravaṇa-pāṭha pair."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Verse 15","title":"Yoga and knowledge are inseparable","sa":["योगो हि ज्ञानहीनस्तु न क्षमो मोक्षकर्मणि ।","तस्माज्ज्ञानं च योगं च मुमुक्षुर्दृढमभ्यसेत् ॥"],"iast":["yogo hi jñāna-hīnastu na kṣamo mokṣa-karmaṇi |","tasmāj jñānaṃ ca yogaṃ ca mumukṣur dṛḍham abhyaset ||"],"words":[["yogaḥ","m. nom. sg.","yoga"],["hi","particle","indeed"],["jñāna-hīnaḥ","cmpd. m. nom. sg.","devoid of knowledge"],["tu","particle","but"],["na kṣamaḥ","neg. + m. nom. sg.","not competent"],["mokṣa-karmaṇi","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the act of liberation"],["tasmāt","adv.","therefore"],["jñānam","n. acc. sg.","knowledge"],["yogam","m. acc. sg.","yoga"],["mumukṣuḥ","m. nom. sg.","one desiring liberation"],["dṛḍham","adv.","firmly"],["abhyaset","3rd sg. opt.","should practice"]],"lit":"Yoga indeed, devoid of knowledge, is not competent for the act of liberation. Therefore, the seeker of liberation should firmly practice both knowledge and yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A key philosophical statement. Yoga alone (without jñāna) does not liberate; jñāna alone (without yoga) does not liberate. They are complementary, not substitutes. This is the Yoga Tattva's position on the karma-jñāna debate: do both."]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"The four yogas","sa":["योगो हि बहुधा ब्रह्मन्भिद्यते व्यवहारतः ।","मन्त्रयोगो लयश्चैव हठोऽसौ राजयोगतः ॥"],"iast":["yogo hi bahudhā brahman bhidyate vyavahārataḥ |","mantra-yogo layaścaiva haṭho 'sau rāja-yogataḥ ||"],"words":[["yogaḥ","m. nom. sg.","yoga"],["hi","particle","indeed"],["bahudhā","adv.","manifoldly"],["brahman","voc. sg.","O Brahmā"],["bhidyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is divided"],["vyavahārataḥ","adv.","by practice / by usage"],["mantra-yogaḥ","m. nom. sg.","mantra-yoga"],["layaḥ","m. nom. sg.","laya (dissolution)"],["ca eva","particles","and indeed"],["haṭhaḥ asau","—","that haṭha"],["rāja-yogataḥ","adv.","from / through rāja-yoga"]],"lit":"Yoga indeed, O Brahmā, is divided manifoldly by practice — Mantra-yoga, Laya, this Haṭha, and Rāja-yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The locus classicus for the fourfold classification of yoga. Most later texts simply quote this verse. The phrase \"haṭho 'sau\" (\"that haṭha\") suggests the speaker is pointing — Haṭha-yoga is the most practical and is the main subject of the rest of the Upaniṣad."]},{"ref":"Verse 24-25","title":"The eight limbs","sa":["यमश्च नियमश्चैव आसनं प्राणसंयमः ।","प्रत्याहारो धारणा च ध्यानं भ्रूमध्यमे हरिम् ।","समाधिः समतावस्था साष्टाङ्गो योग उच्यते ॥"],"iast":["yamaśca niyamaścaiva āsanaṃ prāṇa-saṃyamaḥ |","pratyāhāro dhāraṇā ca dhyānaṃ bhrūmadhyame harim |","samādhiḥ samatāvasthā sāṣṭāṅgo yoga ucyate ||"],"words":[["yamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","yama (restraints)"],["niyamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","niyama (observances)"],["āsanam","n. nom. sg.","āsana (posture)"],["prāṇa-saṃyamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","prāṇa-control"],["pratyāhāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","pratyāhāra (sense-withdrawal)"],["dhāraṇā","f. nom. sg.","dhāraṇā (concentration)"],["dhyānam","n. nom. sg.","dhyāna (meditation)"],["bhrū-madhyame","n. loc. sg.","between the eyebrows"],["harim","m. acc. sg.","on Hari (= Viṣṇu)"],["samādhiḥ","m. nom. sg.","samādhi"],["samatā-avasthā","f. nom. sg.","the state of equanimity"],["sa-aṣṭāṅgaḥ","m. nom. sg.","eight-limbed"],["ucyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is called"]],"lit":"Yama, niyama, āsana, prāṇa-control, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, and dhyāna on Hari between the eyebrows; samādhi as the state of equanimity — the eight-limbed yoga is so called.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The classical aṣṭāṅga. Note the YT's specification of dhyāna: \"on Hari (Viṣṇu) between the brows.\" Whereas Patañjali leaves dhyāna's object open, the YT (being a Vaiṣṇava-leaning text) specifies it. And samādhi is defined precisely as \"samatā-avasthā\" — the state of equanimity, the equality of jīvātmā and paramātmā."]},{"ref":"Verse 29","title":"The four āsanas","sa":["सिद्धं पद्मं तथा सिंहं भद्रं चेति चतुष्टयम् ॥"],"iast":["siddhaṃ padmaṃ tathā siṃhaṃ bhadraṃ ceti catuṣṭayam ||"],"words":[["siddham","n. acc. sg.","Siddha (perfect)"],["padmam","n. acc. sg.","Padma (lotus)"],["tathā","adv.","thus / also"],["siṃham","n. acc. sg.","Siṃha (lion)"],["bhadram","n. acc. sg.","Bhadra (auspicious)"],["ca iti","particle","and thus"],["catuṣṭayam","n. acc. sg.","the four-fold (set)"]],"lit":"Siddha, Padma, also Siṃha, Bhadra — thus the fourfold (set of āsanas).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva's minimal list of essential āsanas — only four. Later Haṭha-yoga texts dramatically expand this. The YT's position: these four are sufficient."]},{"ref":"Verse 28","title":"The chief yama and niyama","sa":["लघ्वाहारो यमेष्वेको मुख्या भवति नेतरः ।","अहिंसा नियमेष्वेका मुख्या वै चतुरानन ॥"],"iast":["laghv-āhāro yameṣv eko mukhyā bhavati netaraḥ |","ahiṃsā niyameṣv ekā mukhyā vai caturānana ||"],"words":[["laghu-āhāraḥ","cmpd. m. nom. sg.","taking little food"],["yameṣu","m. loc. pl.","among the yamas"],["ekaḥ","m. nom. sg.","one (= alone)"],["mukhyā","adj.","chief"],["bhavati","3rd sg. pres.","is"],["na itaraḥ","neg. + m.","not another"],["ahiṃsā","f. nom. sg.","non-violence"],["niyameṣu","m. loc. pl.","among the niyamas"],["caturānana","voc. sg.","O Four-faced one (= Brahmā)"]],"lit":"Taking little food alone is chief among the yamas; non-violence alone is chief among the niyamas, O Four-faced one.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A bold simplification of the classical Pātañjala framework. Where Patañjali enumerates five yamas (ahiṃsā, satya, asteya, brahmacarya, aparigraha) and five niyamas, the YT reduces them to one each: laghv-āhāra (the chief yama) and ahiṃsā (the chief niyama). This is practical advice: master eating-lightly and harming-no-being — the rest will follow."]},{"ref":"Verse 76","title":"The warning against siddhis","sa":["एते विघ्ना महासिद्धेर्न रमेत्तेषु बुद्धिमान् ।","न दर्शयेत्स्वसामर्थ्यं यस्यकस्यापि योगिराट् ॥"],"iast":["ete vighnā mahā-siddher na rametteṣu buddhimān |","na darśayet sva-sāmarthyaṃ yasya-kasyāpi yogi-rāṭ ||"],"words":[["ete","m. nom. pl.","these"],["vighnāḥ","m. nom. pl.","obstacles"],["mahā-siddheḥ","f. gen. sg.","of the great accomplishment"],["na ramet","3rd sg. opt.","should not delight"],["teṣu","m. loc. pl.","in them"],["buddhimān","m. nom. sg.","the wise one"],["na darśayet","3rd sg. opt.","should not display"],["sva-sāmarthyam","n. acc. sg.","his own capacity / power"],["yasya-kasya api","indef.","to anyone whatsoever"],["yogi-rāṭ","m. nom. sg.","king of yogis"]],"lit":"These are obstacles to the great accomplishment. The wise one should not delight in them. The king of yogis should not display his own powers to anyone whatsoever.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva's ethical center. The siddhis are real and arise naturally — but they are obstacles to mahā-siddhi (the great accomplishment = liberation). They are not the goal; they are dangerous distractions from it. The yogi must consciously refuse them and hide his powers."]},{"ref":"Verse 80","title":"Practice over talk","sa":["अनभ्यासवतश्चैव वृथागोष्ठ्या न सिद्ध्यति ।","तस्मात्सर्वप्रयत्नेन योगमेव सदाभ्यसेत् ॥"],"iast":["anabhyāsa-vataśca eva vṛthā-goṣṭhyā na siddhyati |","tasmāt sarva-prayatnena yogam eva sadā'bhyaset ||"],"words":[["an-abhyāsa-vataḥ","m. gen. sg.","of one without practice"],["eva","particle","indeed"],["vṛthā-goṣṭhyā","f. instr. sg.","by useless conversation"],["na siddhyati","3rd sg. pres.","does not succeed / is not accomplished"],["tasmāt","adv.","therefore"],["sarva-prayatnena","m. instr. sg.","with every effort"],["yogam eva","m. acc. sg.","yoga alone"],["sadā","adv.","always"],["abhyaset","3rd sg. opt.","should practice"]],"lit":"For one without practice, indeed, by useless conversation nothing succeeds. Therefore, with every effort, one should always practice yoga itself.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A pointed admonition. Discussion of yoga is not yoga. Reading about yoga is not yoga. Only practice (abhyāsa) accomplishes anything. This verse is the YT's antidote to spiritual armchair-philosophy."]},{"ref":"Verse 107","title":"Defining Samādhi","sa":["समाधिः समतावस्था जीवात्मपरमात्मनोः ।"],"iast":["samādhiḥ samatāvasthā jīvātma-paramātmanoḥ |"],"words":[["samādhiḥ","m. nom. sg.","samādhi"],["samatā-avasthā","f. nom. sg.","the state of equanimity"],["jīvātma-paramātmanoḥ","cmpd. m. gen. du.","of the individual-Self and the Supreme-Self"]],"lit":"Samādhi is the state of equanimity between the individual self and the supreme Self.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva's precise technical definition of samādhi. Not absorption per se but specifically the equality (samatā) of the two ātmans. This is the highest classical formulation. The yogi in samādhi is jīvanmukta — liberated while embodied."]},{"ref":"Verse 121-122","title":"Mūlabandha and the unification","sa":["प्राणापानौ नादबिन्दू मूलबन्धेन चैकताम् ।","गत्वा योगस्य संसिद्धिं यच्छतो नात्र संशयः ॥"],"iast":["prāṇāpānau nāda-bindū mūla-bandhena caikatām |","gatvā yogasya saṃsiddhiṃ yacchato nātra saṃśayaḥ ||"],"words":[["prāṇa-apānau","m. nom. du.","prāṇa and apāna"],["nāda-bindū","cmpd. nom. du.","nāda (sound) and bindu (point)"],["mūla-bandhena","m. instr. sg.","through the root-lock"],["ca ekatām","f. acc. sg.","and to oneness"],["gatvā","gerund of √gam","having gone"],["yogasya","m. gen. sg.","of yoga"],["saṃsiddhim","f. acc. sg.","the perfection / accomplishment"],["yacchataḥ","pres. part. m. gen.","of one granting / yielding"],["na atra saṃśayaḥ","—","no doubt herein"]],"lit":"Prāṇa and apāna, nāda and bindu — through the root-lock (mūla-bandha), having attained unity, they yield the perfection of yoga. There is no doubt herein.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A central formulation of the unification project of yoga. Four ordinarily-distinct subtle elements — prāṇa (upward life-force), apāna (downward eliminative force), nāda (subtle sound), and bindu (subtle drop/seed) — are brought into unity through mūla-bandha. When they fuse, yoga is complete."]},{"ref":"Verse 138-139","title":"The OM analysis","sa":["अकारे रेचितं पद्ममुकारेणैव भिद्यते ।","मकारे लभते नादमर्धमात्रा तु निश्चला ॥"],"iast":["a-kāre recitaṃ padmam u-kāreṇa eva bhidyate |","ma-kāre labhate nādam ardha-mātrā tu niścalā ||"],"words":[["a-kāre","m. loc. sg.","by the syllable A"],["recitam","p.p. n. acc. sg.","emptied"],["padmam","n. acc. sg.","the lotus"],["u-kāreṇa","m. instr. sg.","by the syllable U"],["eva","particle","indeed"],["bhidyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is pierced"],["ma-kāre","m. loc. sg.","by the syllable M"],["labhate","3rd sg. pres.","one obtains"],["nādam","m. acc. sg.","the [subtle] sound"],["ardha-mātrā","f. nom. sg.","the half-measure (silence after M)"],["tu","particle","but"],["niścalā","f. nom. sg.","motionless"]],"lit":"By the syllable A the [heart-]lotus is emptied; by U it is pierced; by M one obtains the [subtle] sound; by the half-mātrā (the silence after M), one becomes motionless.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva's remarkable analysis of OM applied to the heart-lotus. The four mātrās (A · U · M · silence) correspond to four meditative stages: emptying, piercing, sound-attainment, and stillness. This anticipates the more developed Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad treatment of the four states."]},{"ref":"Verse 140","title":"The Tortoise (Kūrma) Image","sa":["कूर्मः स्वपाणिपादादिशिरश्चात्मनि धारयेत् ।"],"iast":["kūrmaḥ sva-pāṇi-pādādi-śiraścātmani dhārayet |"],"words":[["kūrmaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the tortoise"],["sva-pāṇi-pāda-ādi-śiraḥ","cmpd. n. acc. sg.","his own hands, feet etc., and head"],["ca","particle","and"],["ātmani","m. loc. sg.","into himself"],["dhārayet","3rd sg. opt. caus.","should hold"]],"lit":"Like the tortoise, [the yogi] should hold his own hands, feet, etc., and head within himself.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The image of the kūrma (tortoise) withdrawing limbs into its shell — a Vedic and Upaniṣadic favorite — applied to pratyāhāra. The yogi withdraws all faculties of action and cognition into the Self. The same image appears in Bhagavad Gītā 2.58."]},{"ref":"13. The Opening Invocation","title":"","sa":["योगैश्वर्यं च कैवल्यं जायते यत्प्रसादतः ।","तद्वैष्णवं योगतत्त्वं रामचन्द्रपदं भजे ॥"],"iast":["yogaiśvaryaṃ ca kaivalyaṃ jāyate yatprasādataḥ |","tadvaiṣṇavaṃ yogatattvaṃ rāmacandrapadaṃ bhaje ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From whose grace yogic-lordship and kaivalya arise — that Vaiṣṇava Yoga-Tattva, the feet of Rāmacandra, I worship.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Śānti Mantra","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु मा विद्विषावहै ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ saha nāvavatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai |","tejasvināvadhītam astu mā vidviṣāvahai |","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"OM. May He protect us both; may He nourish us both; may we both work together with great vigor; may our learning be brilliant; may we not hate. OM, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(This is the standard Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda Śānti Mantra — also used with the Taittirīya Upaniṣad and other texts of this Veda.)"]}]},{"t":"14. Verses 1–25 — Setting, Definition of Yoga, the Four Yogas","u":[{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 1","title":"","sa":["योगतत्त्वं प्रवक्ष्यामि योगिनां हितकाम्यया ।","यच्छृत्वा च पठित्वा च सर्वपापैः प्रमुच्यते ।"],"iast":["yogatattvaṃ pravakṣyāmi yogināṃ hitakāmyayā |","yacchṛtvā ca paṭhitvā ca sarvapāpaiḥ pramucyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"I shall expound the principles of yoga (yoga-tattva) for the welfare of yogis — by hearing and reciting which, one is freed from all sins.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 2","title":"","sa":["विष्णुर्नाम महायोगी महाभूतो महातपाः ।","तत्त्वमार्गे यथा दीपो दृश्यते पुरुषोत्तमः ।"],"iast":["viṣṇurnāma mahāyogī mahābhūto mahātapāḥ |","tattvamārge yathā dīpo dṛśyate puruṣottamaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Viṣṇu, the great Yogī, the Great Being, the great Tapas-doer, shines on the path of truth (tattva) like a lamp — the Supreme Person.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 3","title":"","sa":["तमाराध्य जगन्नाथं प्रणिपत्य पितामहः ।","पप्रच्छ योगतत्त्वं मे ब्रूहि चाष्टाङ्गसंयुतम् ।"],"iast":["tamārādhya jagannāthaṃ praṇipatya pitāmahaḥ |","papraccha yogatattvaṃ me brūhi cāṣṭāṅgasaṃyutam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Pitāmaha (Brahmā), having approached and prostrated to that Lord of the universe, asked: 'Tell me the yoga-tattva together with its eight limbs.'","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 4","title":"","sa":["तमुवाच हृषीकेशो वक्ष्यामि शृणु तत्त्वतः ।","सर्वे जीवाः सुखैर्दुखैर्मायाजालेन वेष्टिताः ।"],"iast":["tamuvāca hṛṣīkeśo vakṣyāmi śṛṇu tattvataḥ |","sarve jīvāḥ sukhairdukhairmāyājālena veṣṭitāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"To him Hṛṣīkeśa (Viṣṇu) said: 'I shall declare it; listen as it really is. All living beings are bound by the net of māyā with pleasures and pains.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 5","title":"","sa":["तेषां मुक्तिकरं मार्गं मायाजालनिकृन्तनम् ।","जन्ममृत्युजराव्याधिनाशनं मृत्युतारकम् ।"],"iast":["teṣāṃ muktikaraṃ mārgaṃ māyājālanikṛntanam |","janmamṛtyujarāvyādhināśanaṃ mṛtyutārakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"I shall tell you the path that grants liberation to them — that cuts asunder the net of māyā, destroys birth-death-old-age-disease, and carries one across death.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 6","title":"","sa":["नानामार्गैस्तु दुष्प्रापं कैवल्यं परमं पदम् ।","पतिताः शास्त्रजालेषु प्रज्ञया तेन मोहिताः ।"],"iast":["nānāmārgaistu duṣprāpaṃ kaivalyaṃ paramaṃ padam |","patitāḥ śāstrajāleṣu prajñayā tena mohitāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"By manifold other paths, it (kaivalya, the supreme state) is hard to attain. Many have fallen into the net of scriptures and have been deluded by their own intellect.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 7","title":"","sa":["अनिर्वाच्यं पदं वक्तुं न शक्यं तैः सुरैरपि ।","स्वात्मप्रकाशरूपं तत्किं शास्त्रेण प्रकाशते ।"],"iast":["anirvācyaṃ padaṃ vaktuṃ na śakyaṃ taiḥ surairapi |","svātmaprakāśarūpaṃ tatkiṃ śāstreṇa prakāśate |"],"words":[],"lit":"The state which cannot be uttered — even the gods cannot speak of it. How can what is self-luminous Self-revelation be illumined by mere scripture?","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 8","title":"","sa":["निष्कलं निर्मलं शान्तं सर्वातीतं निरामयम् ।","तदेव जीवरूपेण पुण्यपापफलैर्वृतम् ।"],"iast":["niṣkalaṃ nirmalaṃ śāntaṃ sarvātītaṃ nirāmayam |","tadeva jīvarūpeṇa puṇyapāpaphalairvṛtam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Itself stainless, pure, peaceful, beyond all, free from disease — that very thing, in the form of the jīva, has been enveloped by the fruits of merit and demerit.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 9","title":"","sa":["परमात्मपदं नित्यं तत्कथं जीवतां गतम् ।","सर्वभावपदातीतं ज्ञानरूपं निरञ्जनम् ।"],"iast":["paramātmapadaṃ nityaṃ tatkathaṃ jīvatāṃ gatam |","sarvabhāvapadātītaṃ jñānarūpaṃ nirañjanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"How has the eternal abode of the supreme Self attained jīva-hood? It is beyond all conditions of being; it is of the form of knowledge; without taint.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 10","title":"","sa":["वारिवत्स्फुरितं तस्मिंस्तत्राहंकृतिरुत्थिता ।","पञ्चात्मकमभूत्पिण्डं धातुबद्धं गुणात्मकम् ।"],"iast":["vārivatsphuritaṃ tasmiṃstatrāhaṃkṛtirutthitā |","pañcātmakamabhūtpiṇḍaṃ dhātubaddhaṃ guṇātmakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Therein, like a ripple in water, the ego-sense arose. The body, five-fold in nature, bound by elements, made of guṇas, came to be.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 11","title":"","sa":["सुखदुःखैः समायुक्तं जीवभावनया कुरु ।","तेन जीवाभिधा प्रोक्ता विशुद्धैः परमात्मनि ।"],"iast":["sukhaduḥkhaiḥ samāyuktaṃ jīvabhāvanayā kuru |","tena jīvābhidhā proktā viśuddhaiḥ paramātmani |"],"words":[],"lit":"Joined with pleasures and pains by the act of jīva-cognition, it is so designated as 'jīva' by the pure ones in the Supreme Self.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 12","title":"","sa":["कामक्रोधभयं चापि मोहलोभमदो रजः ।","जन्ममृत्युश्च कार्पण्यं शोकस्तन्द्रा क्षुधा तृषा ।"],"iast":["kāmakrodhabhayaṃ cāpi mohalobhamado rajaḥ |","janmamṛtyuśca kārpaṇyaṃ śokastandrā kṣudhā tṛṣā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Desire, anger, fear too, delusion, greed, pride, passion; birth, death, miserliness, sorrow, lethargy, hunger, thirst —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 13","title":"","sa":["तृष्णा लज्जा भयं दुह्खं विषादो हर्ष एव च ।","एभिर्दोषैर्विनिर्मुक्तः स जीवः केवलो मतः ।"],"iast":["tṛṣṇā lajjā bhayaṃ duhkhaṃ viṣādo harṣa eva ca |","ebhirdoṣairvinirmuktaḥ sa jīvaḥ kevalo mataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Craving, shame, fear, sorrow, dejection, and joy too — freed from these defects, that jīva is considered kevala (alone, liberated).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 14","title":"","sa":["तस्माद्दोषविनाशार्थमुपायं कथयामि ते ।","योगहीनं कथं ज्ञानं मोक्षदं भवति ध्रुवम् ।"],"iast":["tasmāddoṣavināśārthamupāyaṃ kathayāmi te |","yogahīnaṃ kathaṃ jñānaṃ mokṣadaṃ bhavati dhruvam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Therefore, for the destruction of these defects, I declare the means to you. How can knowledge devoid of yoga surely grant liberation?","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 15","title":"","sa":["योगो हि ज्ञानहीनस्तु न क्षमो मोक्षकर्मणि ।","तस्माज्ज्ञानं च योगं च मुमुक्षुर्दृढमभ्यसेत् ।"],"iast":["yogo hi jñānahīnastu na kṣamo mokṣakarmaṇi |","tasmājjñānaṃ ca yogaṃ ca mumukṣurdṛḍhamabhyaset |"],"words":[],"lit":"Yoga, devoid of knowledge, is not competent for the act of liberation. Therefore, the seeker of liberation should firmly practice both knowledge and yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 16","title":"","sa":["अज्ञानादेव संसारो ज्ञानादेव विमुच्यते ।","ज्ञानस्वरूपमेवादौ ज्ञानं ज्ञेयैकसाधनम् ।"],"iast":["ajñānādeva saṃsāro jñānādeva vimucyate |","jñānasvarūpamevādau jñānaṃ jñeyaikasādhanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"From ignorance alone is saṃsāra; from knowledge alone is one liberated. Knowledge by its very nature is, at first, the sole means to the knowable.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 17","title":"","sa":["ज्ञातं येन निजं रूपं कैवल्यं परमं पदम् ।","निष्कलं निर्मलं साक्षात्सच्चिदानन्दरूपकम् ।"],"iast":["jñātaṃ yena nijaṃ rūpaṃ kaivalyaṃ paramaṃ padam |","niṣkalaṃ nirmalaṃ sākṣātsaccidānandarūpakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"By whom the own form is known — that is kaivalya, the supreme state — stainless, pure, directly experienced, of the form of being-consciousness-bliss,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 18","title":"","sa":["उत्पत्तिस्थितिसंहारस्फूर्तिज्ञानविवर्जितम् ।","एतज्ज्ञानमिति प्रोक्तमथ योगं ब्रवीमि ते ।"],"iast":["utpattisthitisaṃhārasphūrtijñānavivarjitam |","etajjñānamiti proktamatha yogaṃ bravīmi te |"],"words":[],"lit":"Devoid of the flashings of arising, sustaining, and withdrawal — this is called knowledge. Now I shall tell you of yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 19","title":"","sa":["योगो हि बहुधा ब्रह्मन्भिद्यते व्यवहारतः ।","मन्त्रयोगो लयश्चैव हठोऽसौ राजयोगतः ।"],"iast":["yogo hi bahudhā brahmanbhidyate vyavahārataḥ |","mantrayogo layaścaiva haṭho'sau rājayogataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Yoga indeed, O Brahmā, is divided manifoldly by practice — Mantra-yoga, Laya-yoga, Haṭha-yoga, and Rāja-yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 20","title":"","sa":["आरम्भश्च घटश्चैव तथा परिचयः स्मृतः ।","निष्पत्तिश्चेत्यवस्था च सर्वत्र परिकीर्तिता ।"],"iast":["ārambhaśca ghaṭaścaiva tathā paricayaḥ smṛtaḥ |","niṣpattiścetyavasthā ca sarvatra parikīrtitā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Four states are universally proclaimed: Ārambha (beginning), Ghaṭa (vessel), Paricaya (acquaintance), and Niṣpatti (completion).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 21","title":"","sa":["एतेषां लक्षणं ब्रह्मन्वक्ष्ये शृणु समासतः ।","मातृकादियुतं मन्त्रं द्वादशाब्दं तु यो जपेत् ।"],"iast":["eteṣāṃ lakṣaṇaṃ brahmanvakṣye śṛṇu samāsataḥ |","mātṛkādiyutaṃ mantraṃ dvādaśābdaṃ tu yo japet |"],"words":[],"lit":"I shall tell you the characteristics of these in brief, O Brahmā; listen. He who repeats a mantra united with mātṛkā (the alphabet) for twelve years —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 22","title":"","sa":["क्रमेण लभते ज्ञानमणिमादिगुणान्वितम् ।","अल्पबुद्धिरिमं योगं सेवते साधकाधमः ।"],"iast":["krameṇa labhate jñānamaṇimādiguṇānvitam |","alpabuddhirimaṃ yogaṃ sevate sādhakādhamaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Gradually obtains knowledge endowed with the qualities of aṇimā (minuteness) etc. The dull-witted, lowest of seekers, resorts to this yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 23","title":"","sa":["लययोगश्चित्तलयः कोटिशः परिकीर्तितः ।","गच्छंस्तिष्ठन्स्वपन्भुञ्जन्ध्यायेन्निष्कलमीश्वरम् ।"],"iast":["layayogaścittalayaḥ koṭiśaḥ parikīrtitaḥ |","gacchaṃstiṣṭhansvapanbhuñjandhyāyenniṣkalamīśvaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"Laya-yoga is the dissolution of citta, declared in crores of ways. While walking, standing, sleeping, eating — one should meditate on the partless Lord.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 24","title":"","sa":["स एव लययोगः स्याद्धठयोगमतः शृणु ।","यमश्च नियमश्चैव आसनं प्राणसंयमः ।"],"iast":["sa eva layayogaḥ syāddhaṭhayogamataḥ śṛṇu |","yamaśca niyamaścaiva āsanaṃ prāṇasaṃyamaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"That alone is Laya-yoga. Now hear of Haṭha-yoga. Yama, niyama too, āsana, prāṇa-restraint,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"14. Verses 1–25 · 25","title":"","sa":["प्रत्याहारो धारणा च ध्यानं भ्रूमध्यमे हरिम् ।","समाधिः समतावस्था साष्टाङ्गो योग उच्यते ।"],"iast":["pratyāhāro dhāraṇā ca dhyānaṃ bhrūmadhyame harim |","samādhiḥ samatāvasthā sāṣṭāṅgo yoga ucyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"Pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā too, dhyāna of Hari between the brows, and samādhi as the state of equanimity — yoga of eight limbs is so called.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"15. Verses 26–49 — Eight Limbs, Mudrās, Preparation","u":[{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 26","title":"","sa":["महामुद्रा महाबन्धो महावेधश्च खेचरी ।","जालन्धरोड्डियाणश्च मूलबन्धैस्तथैव च ।"],"iast":["mahāmudrā mahābandho mahāvedhaśca khecarī |","jālandharoḍḍiyāṇaśca mūlabandhaistathaiva ca |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mahāmudrā, mahābandha, mahāvedha too, and khecarī; jālandhara, uḍḍiyāna, and the mūlabandha too,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 27","title":"","sa":["दीर्घप्रणवसन्धानं सिद्धान्तश्रवणं परम् ।","वज्रोली चामरोली च सहजोली त्रिधा मता ।"],"iast":["dīrghapraṇavasandhānaṃ siddhāntaśravaṇaṃ param |","vajrolī cāmarolī ca sahajolī tridhā matā |"],"words":[],"lit":"The contemplation of the prolonged praṇava (OM), hearing the supreme conclusion (siddhānta); vajrolī, amarolī, and sahajolī — known as threefold.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 28","title":"","sa":["एतेषां लक्षणं ब्रह्मन्प्रत्येकं शृणु तत्त्वतः ।","लघ्वाहारो यमेष्वेको मुख्या भवति नेतरः ।"],"iast":["eteṣāṃ lakṣaṇaṃ brahmanpratyekaṃ śṛṇu tattvataḥ |","laghvāhāro yameṣveko mukhyā bhavati netaraḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Of these, O Brahmā, I shall declare the characteristics — one by one — as they truly are; listen. Among the yamas, taking little food (laghu-āhāra) alone is the chief; not the others.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 29","title":"","sa":["अहिंसा नियमेष्वेका मुख्या वै चतुरानन ।","सिद्धं पद्मं तथा सिंहं भद्रं चेति चतुष्टयम् ।"],"iast":["ahiṃsā niyameṣvekā mukhyā vai caturānana |","siddhaṃ padmaṃ tathā siṃhaṃ bhadraṃ ceti catuṣṭayam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Among the niyamas, non-violence alone is chief, O Four-faced one. Siddhāsana, Padmāsana, Siṃhāsana, and Bhadrāsana — these four (āsanas).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 30","title":"","sa":["प्रथमाभ्यासकाले तु विघ्नाः स्युश्चतुरानन ।","आलस्यं कत्थनं धूर्तगोष्ठी मन्त्रादिसाधनम् ।"],"iast":["prathamābhyāsakāle tu vighnāḥ syuścaturānana |","ālasyaṃ katthanaṃ dhūrtagoṣṭhī mantrādisādhanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"At the time of beginning the practice, O Four-faced one, obstacles arise: laziness, boasting, fellowship with knaves, mantra-practice, etc.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 31","title":"","sa":["धातुस्त्रीलौल्यकादीनि मृगतृष्णामयानि वै ।","ज्ञात्वा सुधीस्त्यजेत्सर्वान्विघ्नान्पुण्यप्रभावतः ।"],"iast":["dhātustrīlaulyakādīni mṛgatṛṣṇāmayāni vai |","jñātvā sudhīstyajetsarvānvighnānpuṇyaprabhāvataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mineral pursuits, lust for women, etc. — these are like mirages. Knowing them, the wise one should give up all obstacles through the power of merit.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 32","title":"","sa":["प्राणायामं ततः कुर्यात्पद्मासनगतः स्वयम् ।","सुशोभनं मठं कुर्यात्सूक्ष्मद्वारं तु निर्व्रणम् ।"],"iast":["prāṇāyāmaṃ tataḥ kuryātpadmāsanagataḥ svayam |","suśobhanaṃ maṭhaṃ kuryātsūkṣmadvāraṃ tu nirvraṇam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then, seated in Padmāsana, one should practice prāṇāyāma by oneself. One should build a well-decorated hut with a small door, free of cracks,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 33","title":"","sa":["सुष्ठु लिप्तं गोमयेन सुधया वा प्रयत्नतः ।","मत्कुणैर्मशकैर्लूतैर्वर्जितं च प्रयत्नतः ।"],"iast":["suṣṭhu liptaṃ gomayena sudhayā vā prayatnataḥ |","matkuṇairmaśakairlūtairvarjitaṃ ca prayatnataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Well-smeared with cow-dung or with mortar carefully; entirely free of bedbugs, mosquitoes, and spiders carefully;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 34","title":"","sa":["दिने दिने च संमृष्टं संमार्जन्या विशेषतः ।","वासितं च सुगन्धेन धूपितं गुग्गुलादिभिः ।"],"iast":["dine dine ca saṃmṛṣṭaṃ saṃmārjanyā viśeṣataḥ |","vāsitaṃ ca sugandhena dhūpitaṃ guggulādibhiḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Cleaned every day with a broom particularly; perfumed with fragrance and fumigated with bdellium etc.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 35","title":"","sa":["नात्युच्छ्रितं नातिनीचं चैलाजिनकुशोत्तरम् ।","तत्रोपविश्य मेधावी पद्मासनसमन्वितः ।"],"iast":["nātyucchritaṃ nātinīcaṃ cailājinakuśottaram |","tatropaviśya medhāvī padmāsanasamanvitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Not too high nor too low — with cloth, skin, and kuśa-grass on top. There the wise one, having seated himself in padmāsana posture,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 36","title":"","sa":["ऋजुकायः प्राञ्जलिश्च प्रणमेदिष्टदेवताम् ।","ततो दक्षिणहस्तस्य अङ्गुष्ठेनैव पिङ्गलाम् ।"],"iast":["ṛjukāyaḥ prāñjaliśca praṇamediṣṭadevatām |","tato dakṣiṇahastasya aṅguṣṭhenaiva piṅgalām |"],"words":[],"lit":"Body erect, hands folded, should bow to the chosen deity. Then with the thumb of the right hand on the piṅgalā (right nostril),","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 37","title":"","sa":["निरुध्य पूरयेद्वायुमिडया तु शनैः शनैः ।","यथाशक्त्यविरोधेन ततः कुर्याच्च कुम्भकम् ।"],"iast":["nirudhya pūrayedvāyumiḍayā tu śanaiḥ śanaiḥ |","yathāśaktyavirodhena tataḥ kuryācca kumbhakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having blocked it, he should fill in air through iḍā (left nostril) slowly, slowly; in accordance with capacity and without obstruction — then he should do kumbhaka (retention).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 38","title":"","sa":["पुनस्त्यजेत्पिङ्गलया शनैरेव न वेगतः ।","पुनः पिङ्गलयापूर्य पूरयेदुदरं शनैः ।"],"iast":["punastyajetpiṅgalayā śanaireva na vegataḥ |","punaḥ piṅgalayāpūrya pūrayedudaraṃ śanaiḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then he should release through piṅgalā, slowly only, not forcibly. Again filling through piṅgalā, he should fill the belly slowly.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 39","title":"","sa":["धारयित्वा यथाशक्ति रेचयेदिडया शनैः ।","यया त्यजेत्तयापूर्य धारयेदविरोधतः ।"],"iast":["dhārayitvā yathāśakti recayediḍayā śanaiḥ |","yayā tyajettayāpūrya dhārayedavirodhataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having held in accordance with capacity, he should release through iḍā slowly. As one releases by which, by that one should fill, then hold without obstruction.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 40","title":"","sa":["जानु प्रदक्षिणीकृत्य न द्रुतं न विलम्बितम् ।","अङ्गुलिस्फोटनं कुर्यात्सा मात्रा परिगीयते ।"],"iast":["jānu pradakṣiṇīkṛtya na drutaṃ na vilambitam |","aṅgulisphoṭanaṃ kuryātsā mātrā parigīyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"Going round one's knee — not too quick, not too slow — one should snap the fingers; that is the measure (mātrā) sung of.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 41","title":"","sa":["इडया वायुमारोप्य शनैः षोडशमात्रया ।","कुम्भयेत्पूरितं पश्चाच्चतुःषष्ट्या तु मात्रया ।"],"iast":["iḍayā vāyumāropya śanaiḥ ṣoḍaśamātrayā |","kumbhayetpūritaṃ paścāccatuḥṣaṣṭyā tu mātrayā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having drawn up the air through iḍā slowly, by sixteen mātrās; should retain the filled (air) afterward by sixty-four mātrās;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 42","title":"","sa":["रेचयेत्पिङ्गलानाड्या द्वात्रिंशन्मात्रया पुनः ।","पुनः पिङ्गलयापूर्य पूर्ववत्सुसमाहितः ।"],"iast":["recayetpiṅgalānāḍyā dvātriṃśanmātrayā punaḥ |","punaḥ piṅgalayāpūrya pūrvavatsusamāhitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Should release through piṅgalā-nāḍī by thirty-two mātrās again; having filled again through piṅgalā, fully concentrated as before.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 43","title":"","sa":["प्रातर्मध्यन्दिने सायमर्धरात्रे च कुम्भकान् ।","शनैरशीतिपर्यन्तं चतुर्वारं समभ्यसेत् ।"],"iast":["prātarmadhyandine sāyamardharātre ca kumbhakān |","śanairaśītiparyantaṃ caturvāraṃ samabhyaset |"],"words":[],"lit":"Morning, noon, evening, and midnight too — these kumbhakas; gradually up to eighty (counts) — four times he should practice.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 44","title":"","sa":["एवं मासत्रयाभ्यासान्नाडीशुद्धिस्ततो भवेत् ।","यदा तु नाडीशुद्धिः स्यात्तदा चिह्नानि बाह्यतः ।"],"iast":["evaṃ māsatrayābhyāsānnāḍīśuddhistato bhavet |","yadā tu nāḍīśuddhiḥ syāttadā cihnāni bāhyataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Thus, by three months' practice, purification of nāḍīs occurs. When there is purification of nāḍīs, then signs (cihnāni) externally —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 45","title":"","sa":["जायन्ते योगिनो देहे तानि वक्ष्याम्यशेषतः ।","शरीरलघुता दीप्तिर्जाठराग्निविवर्धनम् ।"],"iast":["jāyante yogino dehe tāni vakṣyāmyaśeṣataḥ |","śarīralaghutā dīptirjāṭharāgnivivardhanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Appear in the yogi's body; these I shall declare without remainder. Lightness of body, brightness, increase of the digestive fire,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 46","title":"","sa":["कृशत्वं च शरीरस्य तदा जायेत निश्चितम् ।","योगाविघ्नकराहारं वर्जयेद्योगवित्तमः ।"],"iast":["kṛśatvaṃ ca śarīrasya tadā jāyeta niścitam |","yogāvighnakarāhāraṃ varjayedyogavittamaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"And leanness of body — this surely arises then. The yoga-knower should avoid food that obstructs yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 47","title":"","sa":["लवणं सर्षपं चाम्लमुष्णं रूक्षं च तीक्ष्णकम् ।","शाकजातं रामठादि वह्निस्त्रीपथसेवनम् ।"],"iast":["lavaṇaṃ sarṣapaṃ cāmlamuṣṇaṃ rūkṣaṃ ca tīkṣṇakam |","śākajātaṃ rāmaṭhādi vahnistrīpathasevanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Salt, mustard, sour, hot, dry, and pungent; vegetable-products, asafoetida etc., fire (= excessive heat), women, the path (= traveling),","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 48","title":"","sa":["प्रातःस्नानोपवासादिकायक्लेशांश्च वर्जयेत् ।","अभ्यासकाले प्रथमं शस्तं क्षीराज्यभोजनम् ।"],"iast":["prātaḥsnānopavāsādikāyakleśāṃśca varjayet |","abhyāsakāle prathamaṃ śastaṃ kṣīrājyabhojanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Morning bath, fasting and other body-mortifications — one should avoid. At the time of beginning practice, milk and ghee are praiseworthy as food;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"15. Verses 26–49 · 49","title":"","sa":["गोधूममुद्गशाल्यन्नं योगवृद्धिकरं विदुः ।","ततः परं यथेष्टं तु शक्तः स्याद्वायुधारणे ।"],"iast":["godhūmamudgaśālyannaṃ yogavṛddhikaraṃ viduḥ |","tataḥ paraṃ yatheṣṭaṃ tu śaktaḥ syādvāyudhāraṇe |"],"words":[],"lit":"Wheat, mung, and rice — these are known as productive of yoga. After that, as he wishes — he becomes capable of breath-retention.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"16. Verses 50–82 — Stages, Siddhis, Practice","u":[{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 50","title":"","sa":["यथेष्टवायुधारणाद्वायोः सिद्ध्येत्केवलकुम्भकः ।","केवले कुम्भक सिद्धे रेचपूरविवर्जिते ।"],"iast":["yatheṣṭavāyudhāraṇādvāyoḥ siddhyetkevalakumbhakaḥ |","kevale kumbhaka siddhe recapūravivarjite |"],"words":[],"lit":"From holding the air as he wishes, kevala-kumbhaka of the air is accomplished. When kevala-kumbhaka is mastered, free from inhalation and exhalation —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 51","title":"","sa":["न तस्य दुर्लभं किञ्चित्त्रिषु लोकेषु विद्यते ।","प्रस्वेदो जायते पूर्वं मर्दनं तेन कारयेत् ।"],"iast":["na tasya durlabhaṃ kiñcittriṣu lokeṣu vidyate |","prasvedo jāyate pūrvaṃ mardanaṃ tena kārayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Nothing is unattainable for him in the three worlds. Sweat appears first; he should rub it [into the body].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 52","title":"","sa":["ततोऽपि धारणाद्वायोः क्रमेणैव शनैः शनैः ।","कम्पो भवति देहस्य आसनस्थस्य देहिनः ।"],"iast":["tato'pi dhāraṇādvāyoḥ krameṇaiva śanaiḥ śanaiḥ |","kampo bhavati dehasya āsanasthasya dehinaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then, by further holding of the air, gradually, slowly — trembling occurs in the body of the embodied one seated in āsana.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 53","title":"","sa":["ततोऽधिकतराभ्यासाद्दार्दुरी स्वेन जायते ।","यथा च दर्दुरो भाव उत्प्लुन्योत्प्लुत्य गच्छति ।"],"iast":["tato'dhikatarābhyāsāddārdurī svena jāyate |","yathā ca darduro bhāva utplunyotplutya gacchati |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then, by further practice, the 'frog-like (ḍāradurī)' state arises spontaneously. Just as a frog leaps and leaps and goes —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 54","title":"","sa":["पद्मासनस्थितो योगी तथा गच्छति भूतले ।","ततोऽधिकतरभ्यासाद्भूमित्यागश्च जायते ।"],"iast":["padmāsanasthito yogī tathā gacchati bhūtale |","tato'dhikatarabhyāsādbhūmityāgaśca jāyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"So the yogi seated in padmāsana goes along the ground. By still further practice, the abandonment of the ground occurs.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 55","title":"","sa":["पद्मासनस्थ एवासौ भूमिमुत्सृज्य वर्तते ।","अतिमानुषचेष्टादि तथा सामर्थ्यमुद्भवेत् ।"],"iast":["padmāsanastha evāsau bhūmimutsṛjya vartate |","atimānuṣaceṣṭādi tathā sāmarthyamudbhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Padmāsana-seated, he himself rises above the ground and proceeds. Super-human capacities arise as well.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 56","title":"","sa":["न दर्शयेच्च सामर्थ्यं दर्शनं वीर्यवत्तरम् ।","स्वल्पं वा बहुधा दुःखं योगी न व्यथते तदा ।"],"iast":["na darśayecca sāmarthyaṃ darśanaṃ vīryavattaram |","svalpaṃ vā bahudhā duḥkhaṃ yogī na vyathate tadā |"],"words":[],"lit":"He should not show his powers; showing them is harmful to potency. Whether little or much suffering — the yogi is not then afflicted.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 57","title":"","sa":["अल्पमूत्रपुरीषश्च स्वल्पनिद्रश्च जायते ।","कीलवो दृषिका लाला स्वेददुर्गन्धतानने ।"],"iast":["alpamūtrapurīṣaśca svalpanidraśca jāyate |","kīlavo dṛṣikā lālā svedadurgandhatānane |"],"words":[],"lit":"His urine and excrement become little; his sleep also little. Eye-discharge, saliva, sweat, bad odor in the mouth —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 58","title":"","sa":["एतानि सर्वथा तस्य न जायन्ते ततः परम् ।","ततोऽधिकतराभ्यासाद्बलमुत्पद्यते बहु ।"],"iast":["etāni sarvathā tasya na jāyante tataḥ param |","tato'dhikatarābhyāsādbalamutpadyate bahu |"],"words":[],"lit":"All these no longer arise for him from then on. By yet further practice, much strength is produced —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 59","title":"","sa":["येन भूचर सिद्धिः स्याद्भूचराणां जये क्षमः ।","व्याघ्रो वा शरभो व्यापि गजो गवय एव वा ।"],"iast":["yena bhūcara siddhiḥ syādbhūcarāṇāṃ jaye kṣamaḥ |","vyāghro vā śarabho vyāpi gajo gavaya eva vā |"],"words":[],"lit":"By which the bhūcara-siddhi (control over earth-dwellers) arises; he is capable of conquering creatures of earth: tigers, śarabhas, even elephants, gavayas;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 60","title":"","sa":["सिंहो वा योगिना तेन म्रियन्ते हस्तताडिताः ।","कन्दर्पस्य यथा रूपं तथा स्यादपि योगिनः ।"],"iast":["siṃho vā yoginā tena mriyante hastatāḍitāḥ |","kandarpasya yathā rūpaṃ tathā syādapi yoginaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Or lions — they are slain by him with mere strokes of his hand. Like Kandarpa (Cupid) in form — such becomes the yogi too.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 61","title":"","sa":["तद्रूपवशगा नार्यः काङ्क्षन्ते तस्य सङ्गमम् ।","यदि सङ्गं करोत्येष तस्य बिन्दुक्षयो भवेत् ।"],"iast":["tadrūpavaśagā nāryaḥ kāṅkṣante tasya saṅgamam |","yadi saṅgaṃ karotyeṣa tasya bindukṣayo bhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Subject to his form, women desire his union. If he engages with them, then loss of bindu (vital essence) occurs.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 62","title":"","sa":["वर्जयित्वा स्त्रियाः सङ्गं कुर्यादभ्यासमादरात् ।","योगिनोऽङ्गे सुगन्धश्च जायते बिन्दुधारणात् ।"],"iast":["varjayitvā striyāḥ saṅgaṃ kuryādabhyāsamādarāt |","yogino'ṅge sugandhaśca jāyate bindudhāraṇāt |"],"words":[],"lit":"Avoiding union with women, one should practice with reverence. From holding the bindu, fragrance arises in the yogi's body.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 63","title":"","sa":["ततो रहस्युपाविष्टः प्रणवं प्लुतमात्रया ।","जपेत्पूर्वार्जितानां तु पापानां नाशहेतवे ।"],"iast":["tato rahasyupāviṣṭaḥ praṇavaṃ plutamātrayā |","japetpūrvārjitānāṃ tu pāpānāṃ nāśahetave |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then, having sat in solitude, he should chant the praṇava (OM) with prolonged mātrā — for the destruction of previously-accumulated sins.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 64","title":"","sa":["सर्वविघ्नहरो मन्त्रः प्रणवः सर्वदोषहा ।","एवमभ्यासयोगेन सिद्धिरारम्भसम्भवा ।"],"iast":["sarvavighnaharo mantraḥ praṇavaḥ sarvadoṣahā |","evamabhyāsayogena siddhirārambhasambhavā |"],"words":[],"lit":"The mantra praṇava destroys all obstacles, removes all defects. By practice of yoga in this manner, the perfection of the ārambha (beginning) state arises.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 65","title":"","sa":["ततो भवेद्धठावस्था पवनाभ्यासतत्परा ।","प्राणोऽपानो मनो बुद्धिर्जीवात्मपरमात्मनोः ।"],"iast":["tato bhaveddhaṭhāvasthā pavanābhyāsatatparā |","prāṇo'pāno mano buddhirjīvātmaparamātmanoḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then arises the haṭha-state, devoted to practice of the breath. When prāṇa, apāna, mind, intellect, the individual self, and the supreme Self —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 66","title":"","sa":["अन्योन्यस्याविरोधेन एकता घटते यदा ।","घटावस्थेति सा प्रोक्ता तच्चिह्नानि ब्रवीम्यहम् ।"],"iast":["anyonyasyāvirodhena ekatā ghaṭate yadā |","ghaṭāvastheti sā proktā taccihnāni bravīmyaham |"],"words":[],"lit":"Without mutual opposition come into unity — that is called the ghaṭa-state. Its signs I shall declare to you.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 67","title":"","sa":["पूर्वं यः कथितोऽभ्यासश्चतुर्थांशं परिग्रहेत् ।","दिवा वा यदि वा सायं याममात्रं समभ्यसेत् ।"],"iast":["pūrvaṃ yaḥ kathito'bhyāsaścaturthāṃśaṃ parigrahet |","divā vā yadi vā sāyaṃ yāmamātraṃ samabhyaset |"],"words":[],"lit":"Of the practice declared earlier, he should take a fourth part. Whether by day or evening, he should practice for one yāma (3 hours).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 68","title":"","sa":["एकवारं प्रतिदिनं कुर्यात्केवलकुम्भकम् ।","इन्द्रियाणीन्द्रियार्थेभ्यो यत्प्रत्याहरणं स्फुटम् ।"],"iast":["ekavāraṃ pratidinaṃ kuryātkevalakumbhakam |","indriyāṇīndriyārthebhyo yatpratyāharaṇaṃ sphuṭam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Once per day he should perform kevala-kumbhaka. The clear withdrawal of the senses from sense-objects —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 69","title":"","sa":["योगी कुम्भकमास्थाय प्रत्याहारः स उच्यते ।","यद्यत्पश्यति चक्षुर्भ्यां तत्तदात्मेति भावयेत् ।"],"iast":["yogī kumbhakamāsthāya pratyāhāraḥ sa ucyate |","yadyatpaśyati cakṣurbhyāṃ tattadātmeti bhāvayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"That, established in kumbhaka, is called pratyāhāra. Whatever the eye sees — that he should contemplate as the Self.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 70","title":"","sa":["यद्यच्छृणोति कर्णाभ्यां तत्तदात्मेति भावयेत् ।","लभते नासया यद्यत्तत्तदात्मेति भावयेत् ।"],"iast":["yadyacchṛṇoti karṇābhyāṃ tattadātmeti bhāvayet |","labhate nāsayā yadyattattadātmeti bhāvayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Whatever the ears hear — that he should contemplate as the Self. Whatever the nose smells — that he should contemplate as the Self.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 71","title":"","sa":["जिह्वया यद्रसं ह्यत्ति तत्तदात्मेति भावयेत् ।","त्वचा यद्यत्स्पृशेद्योगी तत्तदात्मेति भावयेत् ।"],"iast":["jihvayā yadrasaṃ hyatti tattadātmeti bhāvayet |","tvacā yadyatspṛśedyogī tattadātmeti bhāvayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Whatever taste the tongue savors — that he should contemplate as the Self. Whatever the yogi touches with the skin — that he should contemplate as the Self.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 72","title":"","sa":["एवं ज्ञानेन्द्रियाणां तु तत्तत्सौख्यं सुसाधयेत् ।","याममात्रं प्रतिदिनं योगी यत्नादतन्द्रितः ।"],"iast":["evaṃ jñānendriyāṇāṃ tu tattatsaukhyaṃ susādhayet |","yāmamātraṃ pratidinaṃ yogī yatnādatandritaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Thus he should well-establish each sense-pleasure as the Self. For one yāma (3 hours) daily, the yogi diligently, undistracted —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 73","title":"","sa":["यथा वा चित्तसामर्थ्यं जायते योगिनो ध्रुवम् ।","दूरश्रुतिर्दूरदृष्टिः क्षणाद्दूरगमस्तथा ।"],"iast":["yathā vā cittasāmarthyaṃ jāyate yogino dhruvam |","dūraśrutirdūradṛṣṭiḥ kṣaṇāddūragamastathā |"],"words":[],"lit":"His mental capacity then surely arises for the yogi: distant hearing, distant vision, swift motion in an instant,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 74","title":"","sa":["वाक्सिद्धिः कामरूपत्वमदृश्यकरणी तथा ।","मलमूत्रप्रलेपेन लोहादेः स्वर्णता भवेत् ।"],"iast":["vāksiddhiḥ kāmarūpatvamadṛśyakaraṇī tathā |","malamūtrapralepena lohādeḥ svarṇatā bhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Power of speech, capacity to change form at will, invisibility; by smearing with excrement and urine, iron and other metals become gold;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 75","title":"","sa":["खे गतिस्तस्य जायेत सन्तताभ्यासयोगतः ।","सदा बुद्धिमता भाव्यं योगिना योगसिद्धये ।"],"iast":["khe gatistasya jāyeta santatābhyāsayogataḥ |","sadā buddhimatā bhāvyaṃ yoginā yogasiddhaye |"],"words":[],"lit":"Movement in the sky arises for him by continuous practice of yoga. Always the wise yogi should be aware, for the perfection of yoga:","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 76","title":"","sa":["एते विघ्ना महासिद्धेर्न रमेत्तेषु बुद्धिमान् ।","न दर्शयेत्स्वसामर्थ्यं यस्यकस्यापि योगिराट् ।"],"iast":["ete vighnā mahāsiddherna rametteṣu buddhimān |","na darśayetsvasāmarthyaṃ yasyakasyāpi yogirāṭ |"],"words":[],"lit":"These (powers) are obstacles to the great accomplishment; the wise one should not delight in them. The chief among yogis should not show his powers to anyone whatsoever.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 77","title":"","sa":["यथा मूढो यथा मूर्खो यथा बधिर एव वा ।","तथा वर्तेत लोकस्य स्वसामर्थ्यस्य गुप्तये ।"],"iast":["yathā mūḍho yathā mūrkho yathā badhira eva vā |","tathā varteta lokasya svasāmarthyasya guptaye |"],"words":[],"lit":"Like a fool, like an idiot, like a deaf person — so should he behave in the world for the concealment of his powers.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 78","title":"","sa":["शिष्याश्च स्वस्वकार्येषु प्रार्थयन्ति न संशयः ।","तत्तत्कर्मकरव्यग्रः स्वाभ्यासेऽविस्मृतो भवेत् ।"],"iast":["śiṣyāśca svasvakāryeṣu prārthayanti na saṃśayaḥ |","tattatkarmakaravyagraḥ svābhyāse'vismṛto bhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"And the disciples too, in their own respective tasks, will request — no doubt. Engaged in their respective tasks, he should not forget his own practice.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 79","title":"","sa":["अविस्मृत्य गुरोर्वाक्यमभ्यसेत्तदहर्निशम् ।","एवं भवेद्धठावस्था सन्तताभ्यासयोगतः ।"],"iast":["avismṛtya gurorvākyamabhyasettadaharniśam |","evaṃ bhaveddhaṭhāvasthā santatābhyāsayogataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Without forgetting the guru's words, he should practice day and night. Thus arises the haṭha-state by continuous practice of yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 80","title":"","sa":["अनभ्यासवतश्चैव वृथागोष्ठ्या न सिद्ध्यति ।","तस्मात्सर्वप्रयत्नेन योगमेव सदाभ्यसेत् ।"],"iast":["anabhyāsavataścaiva vṛthāgoṣṭhyā na siddhyati |","tasmātsarvaprayatnena yogameva sadābhyaset |"],"words":[],"lit":"For one without practice, by mere gossip nothing is accomplished. Therefore by all efforts, one should always practice yoga itself.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 81","title":"","sa":["ततः परिचयावस्था जायतेऽभ्यासयोगतः ।","वायुः परिचितो यत्नादग्निना सह कुण्डलीम् ।"],"iast":["tataḥ paricayāvasthā jāyate'bhyāsayogataḥ |","vāyuḥ paricito yatnādagninā saha kuṇḍalīm |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then arises the paricaya (acquaintance) state through practice of yoga. The air being well-practiced, together with fire, the kuṇḍalī —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"16. Verses 50–82 · 82","title":"","sa":["भावयित्वा सुषुम्नायां प्रविशेदनिरोधतः ।","वायुना सह चित्तं च प्रविशेच्च महापथम् ।"],"iast":["bhāvayitvā suṣumnāyāṃ praviśedanirodhataḥ |","vāyunā saha cittaṃ ca praviśecca mahāpatham |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having been awakened, enters the suṣumnā unobstructed. Together with the air, the mind also enters the great path.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"17. Verses 83–103 — The Five Element Dhāraṇās","u":[{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 83","title":"","sa":["यस्य चित्तं स्वपवनं सुषुम्नां प्रविशेदिह ।","भूमिरापोऽनलो वायुराकाशश्चेति पञ्चकः ।"],"iast":["yasya cittaṃ svapavanaṃ suṣumnāṃ praviśediha |","bhūmirāpo'nalo vāyurākāśaśceti pañcakaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"He whose mind, with his own breath, enters the suṣumnā here — Earth, water, fire, air, ether — these five —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 84","title":"","sa":["येषु पञ्चसु देवानां धारणा पञ्चधोद्यते ।","पादादिजानुपर्यन्तं पृथिवीस्थानमुच्यते ।"],"iast":["yeṣu pañcasu devānāṃ dhāraṇā pañcadhodyate |","pādādijānuparyantaṃ pṛthivīsthānamucyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"In these five deities' dhāraṇā is taught fivefold. From the feet up to the knees, the region of earth is declared.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 85","title":"","sa":["पृथिवी चतुरस्रं च पीतवर्णं लवर्णकम् ।","पार्थिवे वायुमारोप्य लकारेण समन्वितम् ।"],"iast":["pṛthivī caturasraṃ ca pītavarṇaṃ lavarṇakam |","pārthive vāyumāropya lakāreṇa samanvitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Earth is four-sided, yellow-colored, with the syllable la. Having drawn the air into the earth-place with the syllable la accompanied —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 86","title":"","sa":["ध्यायंश्चतुर्भुजाकारं चतुर्वक्त्रं हिरण्मयम् ।","धारयेत्पञ्चघटिकाः पृथिवीजयमाप्नुयात् ।"],"iast":["dhyāyaṃścaturbhujākāraṃ caturvaktraṃ hiraṇmayam |","dhārayetpañcaghaṭikāḥ pṛthivījayamāpnuyāt |"],"words":[],"lit":"Meditating on the four-armed, four-faced golden form (Brahmā), he should hold for five ghaṭikās (= about 2 hours); he obtains conquest of earth.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 87","title":"","sa":["पृथिवीयोगतो मृत्युर्न भवेदस्य योगिनः ।","आजानोः पायुपर्यन्तमपां स्थानं प्रकीर्तितम् ।"],"iast":["pṛthivīyogato mṛtyurna bhavedasya yoginaḥ |","ājānoḥ pāyuparyantamapāṃ sthānaṃ prakīrtitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"By yoga with earth, death does not occur for that yogi. From the knees to the anus, the region of water is proclaimed.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 88","title":"","sa":["आपोऽर्धचन्द्रं शुक्लं च वंबीजं परिकीर्तितम् ।","वारुणे वायुमारोप्य वकारेण समन्वितम् ।"],"iast":["āpo'rdhacandraṃ śuklaṃ ca vaṃbījaṃ parikīrtitam |","vāruṇe vāyumāropya vakāreṇa samanvitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Water is half-moon shape, white, the syllable VAṂ is proclaimed. Having drawn the air into the water-place with the syllable va accompanied —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 89","title":"","sa":["स्मरन्नारायणं देवं चतुर्बाहुं किरीटिनम् ।","शुद्धस्फटिकसङ्काशं पीतवाससमच्युतम् ।"],"iast":["smarannārāyaṇaṃ devaṃ caturbāhuṃ kirīṭinam |","śuddhasphaṭikasaṅkāśaṃ pītavāsasamacyutam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Remembering the deity Nārāyaṇa, four-armed, crowned, of the appearance of pure crystal, yellow-clad, imperishable,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 90","title":"","sa":["धारयेत्पञ्चघटिकाः सर्वपापैः प्रमुच्यते ।","ततो जलाद्भयं नास्ति जले मृत्युर्न विद्यते ।"],"iast":["dhārayetpañcaghaṭikāḥ sarvapāpaiḥ pramucyate |","tato jalādbhayaṃ nāsti jale mṛtyurna vidyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"He should hold for five ghaṭikās; he is freed from all sins. Then there is no fear from water; in water, death does not occur.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 91","title":"","sa":["आपायोर्हृदयान्तं च वह्निस्थानं प्रकीर्तितम् ।","वह्निस्त्रिकोणं रक्तं च रेफाक्षरसमुद्भवम् ।"],"iast":["āpāyorhṛdayāntaṃ ca vahnisthānaṃ prakīrtitam |","vahnistrikoṇaṃ raktaṃ ca rephākṣarasamudbhavam |"],"words":[],"lit":"From water (anus) to heart, the region of fire is proclaimed. Fire is triangular, red, arisen from the syllable ra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 92","title":"","sa":["वह्नौ चानिलमारोप्य रेफाक्षरसमुज्ज्वलम् ।","त्रियक्षं वरदं रुद्रं तरुणादित्यसंनिभम् ।"],"iast":["vahnau cānilamāropya rephākṣarasamujjvalam |","triyakṣaṃ varadaṃ rudraṃ taruṇādityasaṃnibham |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having drawn the air into fire (the fire region) with the syllable ra brilliant; (meditating on) the three-eyed, boon-giving Rudra, of the appearance of the young sun,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 93","title":"","sa":["भस्मोद्धूलितसर्वाङ्गं सुप्रसन्नमनुस्मरन् ।","धारयेत्पञ्चघटिका वह्निनासौ न दाह्यते ।"],"iast":["bhasmoddhūlitasarvāṅgaṃ suprasannamanusmaran |","dhārayetpañcaghaṭikā vahnināsau na dāhyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"All-limbs-smeared-with-ash, very pleasant — remembering thus, he should hold for five ghaṭikās; he is not burnt by fire.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 94","title":"","sa":["न दह्यते शरीरं च प्रविष्टस्याग्निमण्डले ।","आहृदयाद्भ्रुवोर्मध्यं वायुस्थानं प्रकीर्तितम् ।"],"iast":["na dahyate śarīraṃ ca praviṣṭasyāgnimaṇḍale |","āhṛdayādbhruvormadhyaṃ vāyusthānaṃ prakīrtitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"The body is not burnt for one who has entered the fire-circle. From the heart to between the eyebrows, the region of air is proclaimed.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 95","title":"","sa":["वायुः षट्कोणकं कृष्णं यकाराक्षरभासुरम् ।","मारुतं मरुतां स्थाने यकाराक्षरभासुरम् ।"],"iast":["vāyuḥ ṣaṭkoṇakaṃ kṛṣṇaṃ yakārākṣarabhāsuram |","mārutaṃ marutāṃ sthāne yakārākṣarabhāsuram |"],"words":[],"lit":"Air is six-sided, black, brilliant with the syllable ya. Maruts (winds) are in the region of air, brilliant with the syllable ya.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 96","title":"","sa":["धारयेत्तत्र सर्वज्ञमीश्वरं विश्वतोमुखम् ।","धारयेत्पञ्चघटिका वायुवद्व्योमगो भवेत् ।"],"iast":["dhārayettatra sarvajñamīśvaraṃ viśvatomukham |","dhārayetpañcaghaṭikā vāyuvadvyomago bhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"There, holding on the omniscient Lord, all-faced — he should hold for five ghaṭikās; he moves through the ether like the wind.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 97","title":"","sa":["मरणं न तु वायोश्च भयं भवति योगिनः ।","आभ्रूमध्यात्तु मूर्धान्तमाकाशस्थानमुच्यते ।"],"iast":["maraṇaṃ na tu vāyośca bhayaṃ bhavati yoginaḥ |","ābhrūmadhyāttu mūrdhāntamākāśasthānamucyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"There is no death from air, nor fear for the yogi. From between the eyebrows up to the crown of the head, the region of ether is proclaimed.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 98","title":"","sa":["व्योम वृत्तं च धूम्रं च हकाराक्षरभासुरम् ।","आकाशे वायुमारोप्य हकारोपरि शङ्करम् ।"],"iast":["vyoma vṛttaṃ ca dhūmraṃ ca hakārākṣarabhāsuram |","ākāśe vāyumāropya hakāropari śaṅkaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"Space is circular, smoky, brilliant with the syllable ha. Having drawn the air into space with the syllable ha — Śaṅkara above,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 99","title":"","sa":["बिन्दुरूपं महादेवं व्योमाकारं सदाशिवम् ।","शुद्धस्फटिकसङ्काशं धृतबालेन्दुमौलिनम् ।"],"iast":["bindurūpaṃ mahādevaṃ vyomākāraṃ sadāśivam |","śuddhasphaṭikasaṅkāśaṃ dhṛtabālendumaulinam |"],"words":[],"lit":"(Meditating on) the great deity in the form of a bindu (point), of the form of space, the eternally auspicious — of the appearance of pure crystal, bearing the young moon on his crest,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 100","title":"","sa":["पञ्चवक्त्रयुतं सौम्यं दशबाहुं त्रिलोचनम् ।","सर्वायुधैर्धृताकारं सर्वभूषणभूषितम् ।"],"iast":["pañcavaktrayutaṃ saumyaṃ daśabāhuṃ trilocanam |","sarvāyudhairdhṛtākāraṃ sarvabhūṣaṇabhūṣitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Five-faced, gentle, ten-armed, three-eyed, of the form of all weapons, adorned with all ornaments;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 101","title":"","sa":["उमार्धदेहं वरदं सर्वकारणकारणम् ।","आकाशधारणात्तस्य खेचरत्वं भवेद्ध्रुवम् ।"],"iast":["umārdhadehaṃ varadaṃ sarvakāraṇakāraṇam |","ākāśadhāraṇāttasya khecaratvaṃ bhaveddhruvam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Half-bodied with Umā, boon-giving, the cause of all causes. By ether-dhāraṇā, sky-walking (khecaratva) surely arises for him.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 102","title":"","sa":["यत्रकुत्र स्थितो वापि सुखमत्यन्तमश्नुते ।","एवं च धारणाः पञ्च कुर्याद्योगी विचक्षणः ।"],"iast":["yatrakutra sthito vāpi sukhamatyantamaśnute |","evaṃ ca dhāraṇāḥ pañca kuryādyogī vicakṣaṇaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Wherever he is, he experiences extreme happiness. Thus the discerning yogi should perform the five dhāraṇās.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"17. Verses 83–103 · 103","title":"","sa":["ततो दृढशरीरः स्यान्मृत्युस्तस्य न विद्यते ।","ब्रह्मणः प्रलयेनापि न सीदति महामतिः ।"],"iast":["tato dṛḍhaśarīraḥ syānmṛtyustasya na vidyate |","brahmaṇaḥ pralayenāpi na sīdati mahāmatiḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then he has a firm body; for him death does not exist. Even at the dissolution of Brahmā (the cosmic dissolution), the wise one does not perish.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"18. Verses 104–142 — Samādhi, the Seven Mudrās, Final Teaching","u":[{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 104","title":"","sa":["समभ्यसेत्तथा ध्यानं घटिकाषष्टिमेव च ।","वायुं निरुध्य चाकाशे देवतामिष्टदामिति ।"],"iast":["samabhyasettathā dhyānaṃ ghaṭikāṣaṣṭimeva ca |","vāyuṃ nirudhya cākāśe devatāmiṣṭadāmiti |"],"words":[],"lit":"He should likewise practice meditation for sixty ghaṭikās; restraining the air in the ether, [meditating on] the desired deity.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 105","title":"","sa":["सगुणं ध्यानमेतत्स्यादणिमादिगुणप्रदम् ।","निर्गुणध्यानयुक्तस्य समाधिश्च ततो भवेत् ।"],"iast":["saguṇaṃ dhyānametatsyādaṇimādiguṇapradam |","nirguṇadhyānayuktasya samādhiśca tato bhavet |"],"words":[],"lit":"This is meditation with qualities (sa-guṇa), which grants the qualities of aṇimā etc. For one engaged in qualityless (nir-guṇa) meditation, samādhi then arises.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 106","title":"","sa":["दिनद्वादशकेनैव समाधिं समवाप्नुयात् ।","वायुं निरुध्य मेधावी जीवन्मुक्तो भवत्ययम् ।"],"iast":["dinadvādaśakenaiva samādhiṃ samavāpnuyāt |","vāyuṃ nirudhya medhāvī jīvanmukto bhavatyayam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Within twelve days, he obtains samādhi. The wise one, having restrained the air, becomes liberated in life (jīvanmukta).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 107","title":"","sa":["समाधिः समतावस्था जीवात्मपरमात्मनोः ।","यदि स्वदेहमुत्स्रष्टुमिच्छा चेदुत्सृजेत्स्वयम् ।"],"iast":["samādhiḥ samatāvasthā jīvātmaparamātmanoḥ |","yadi svadehamutsraṣṭumicchā cedutsṛjetsvayam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Samādhi is the state of equality between the individual self and the supreme Self. If he wishes to give up his body, he gives it up by himself.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 108","title":"","sa":["परब्रह्मणि लीयेत न तस्योत्क्रान्तिरिष्यते ।","अथ नो चेत्समुत्स्रष्टुं स्वशरीरं प्रियं यदि ।"],"iast":["parabrahmaṇi līyeta na tasyotkrāntiriṣyate |","atha no cetsamutsraṣṭuṃ svaśarīraṃ priyaṃ yadi |"],"words":[],"lit":"He merges into the supreme Brahman; for him, there is no exit (utkrānti, the formal ascent at death). If, however, he does not wish to abandon his dear body —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 109","title":"","sa":["सर्वलोकेषु विहरन्नणिमादिगुणान्वितः ।","कदाचित्स्वेच्छया देवो भूत्वा स्वर्गे महीयते ।"],"iast":["sarvalokeṣu viharannaṇimādiguṇānvitaḥ |","kadācitsvecchayā devo bhūtvā svarge mahīyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"Sporting in all worlds, possessing the qualities of aṇimā etc.; sometimes by his own will, becoming a deva, he is exalted in heaven.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 110","title":"","sa":["मनुष्यो वापि यक्षो वा स्वेच्छयापीक्षणद्भवेत् ।","सिंहो व्याघ्रो गजो वाश्वः स्वेच्छया बहुतामियात् ।"],"iast":["manuṣyo vāpi yakṣo vā svecchayāpīkṣaṇadbhavet |","siṃho vyāghro gajo vāśvaḥ svecchayā bahutāmiyāt |"],"words":[],"lit":"Or by his own will he becomes a human, or a yakṣa, in a flash. Lion, tiger, elephant, or horse — by his own will he assumes many forms.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 111","title":"","sa":["यथेष्टमेव वर्तेत यद्वा योगी महेश्वरः ।","अभ्यासभेदतो भेदः फलं तु सममेव हि ।"],"iast":["yatheṣṭameva varteta yadvā yogī maheśvaraḥ |","abhyāsabhedato bhedaḥ phalaṃ tu samameva hi |"],"words":[],"lit":"He moves as he wishes — wherever, however — the yogi, the Great Lord. The result is the same, though the practices differ.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 112","title":"","sa":["पार्ष्णिं वामस्य पादस्य योनिस्थाने नियोजयेत् ।","प्रसार्य दक्षिणं पादं हस्ताभ्यां धारयेद्दृढम् ।"],"iast":["pārṣṇiṃ vāmasya pādasya yonisthāne niyojayet |","prasārya dakṣiṇaṃ pādaṃ hastābhyāṃ dhārayeddṛḍham |"],"words":[],"lit":"He should place the heel of the left foot at the perineum; extending the right leg, he should hold (it) firmly with both hands.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 113","title":"","sa":["चुबुकं हृदि विन्यस्य पूरयेद्वायुना पुनः ।","कुम्भकेन यथाशक्ति धारयित्वा तु रेचयेत् ।"],"iast":["cubukaṃ hṛdi vinyasya pūrayedvāyunā punaḥ |","kumbhakena yathāśakti dhārayitvā tu recayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Placing the chin on the heart, he should fill with air; having held by kumbhaka in accordance with capacity, he should release.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 114","title":"","sa":["वामाङ्गेन समभ्यस्य दक्षाङ्गेन ततोऽभ्यसेत् ।","प्रसारितस्तु यः पादस्तमूरूपरि नामयेत् ।"],"iast":["vāmāṅgena samabhyasya dakṣāṅgena tato'bhyaset |","prasāritastu yaḥ pādastamūrūpari nāmayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having practiced with the left side, he should then practice with the right side. The leg that is extended — that he should place upon the thigh.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 115","title":"","sa":["अयमेव महाबन्ध उभयत्रैवमभ्यसेत् ।","महाबन्धस्थितो योगी कृत्वा पूरकमेकधीः ।"],"iast":["ayameva mahābandha ubhayatraivamabhyaset |","mahābandhasthito yogī kṛtvā pūrakamekadhīḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"This alone is mahābandha; he should practice it on both sides. The yogi established in mahābandha, having done pūraka, one-pointed —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 116","title":"","sa":["वायुना गतिमावृत्य निभृतं कर्णमुद्रया ।","पुटद्वयं समाक्रम्य वायुः स्फुरति सत्वरम् ।"],"iast":["vāyunā gatimāvṛtya nibhṛtaṃ karṇamudrayā |","puṭadvayaṃ samākramya vāyuḥ sphurati satvaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"Restraining the path of the wind, ears firmly sealed by mudrā; pressing both nostrils, the air rises swiftly upward.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 117","title":"","sa":["अयमेव महावेधः सिद्धैरभ्यस्यतेऽनिशम् ।","अन्तः कपालकुहरे जिह्वां व्यावृत्य धारयेत् ।"],"iast":["ayameva mahāvedhaḥ siddhairabhyasyate'niśam |","antaḥ kapālakuhare jihvāṃ vyāvṛtya dhārayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"This alone is mahāvedha; it is practiced by the perfected ones continuously. Within the cranium-cavity, having turned the tongue back, one should hold it.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 118","title":"","sa":["भ्रूमध्यदृष्टिरप्येषा मुद्रा भवति खेचरी ।","कण्ठमाकुञ्च्य हृदये स्थापयेद्दृढया धिया ।"],"iast":["bhrūmadhyadṛṣṭirapyeṣā mudrā bhavati khecarī |","kaṇṭhamākuñcya hṛdaye sthāpayeddṛḍhayā dhiyā |"],"words":[],"lit":"The gaze between the eyebrows too — this becomes the mudrā called khecarī. Contracting the throat, [placing it] on the heart with firm intellect —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 119","title":"","sa":["बन्धो जालन्धराख्योऽयं मृत्युमातङ्गकेसरी ।","बन्धो येन सुषुम्नायां प्राणस्तूड्डीयते यतः ।"],"iast":["bandho jālandharākhyo'yaṃ mṛtyumātaṅgakesarī |","bandho yena suṣumnāyāṃ prāṇastūḍḍīyate yataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"This is the bandha called jālandhara — a lion against the elephant of death. The bandha by which the prāṇa rises up through the suṣumnā —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 120","title":"","sa":["उड्यानाख्यो हि बन्धोऽयं योगिभिः समुदाहृतः ।","पार्ष्णिभागेन सम्पीड्य योनिमाकुञ्चयेद्दृढम् ।"],"iast":["uḍyānākhyo hi bandho'yaṃ yogibhiḥ samudāhṛtaḥ |","pārṣṇibhāgena sampīḍya yonimākuñcayeddṛḍham |"],"words":[],"lit":"That, called uḍḍiyāna, is so declared by yogis. Pressing the perineum with the heel, contracting the perineum firmly —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 121","title":"","sa":["अपानमूर्ध्वमुत्थाप्य योनिबन्धोऽयमुच्यते ।","प्राणापानौ नादबिन्दू मूलबन्धेन चैकताम् ।"],"iast":["apānamūrdhvamutthāpya yonibandho'yamucyate |","prāṇāpānau nādabindū mūlabandhena caikatām |"],"words":[],"lit":"Drawing the apāna upward — this is called mūla-bandha. Prāṇa and apāna, nāda and bindu, by mūlabandha attain unity.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 122","title":"","sa":["गत्वा योगस्य संसिद्धिं यच्छतो नात्र संशयः ।","करणी विपरीताख्या सर्वव्याधिविनाशिनी ।"],"iast":["gatvā yogasya saṃsiddhiṃ yacchato nātra saṃśayaḥ |","karaṇī viparītākhyā sarvavyādhivināśinī |"],"words":[],"lit":"Attaining the perfection of yoga, controlling [them] — there is no doubt herein. The technique called viparītākaraṇī destroys all diseases.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 123","title":"","sa":["नित्यमभ्यासयुक्तस्य जाठराग्निविवर्धनी ।","आहारो बहुलस्तस्य सम्पाद्यः साधकस्य च ।"],"iast":["nityamabhyāsayuktasya jāṭharāgnivivardhanī |","āhāro bahulastasya sampādyaḥ sādhakasya ca |"],"words":[],"lit":"For one daily engaged in practice, the digestive fire is increased. Plentiful food should be procured for that seeker.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 124","title":"","sa":["अल्पाहारो यदि भवेदग्निर्देहं हरेत्क्षणात् ।","अधःशिरश्चोर्ध्वपादः क्षणं स्यात्प्रथमे दिने ।"],"iast":["alpāhāro yadi bhavedagnirdehaṃ haretkṣaṇāt |","adhaḥśiraścordhvapādaḥ kṣaṇaṃ syātprathame dine |"],"words":[],"lit":"If the food is little, the fire would consume the body in an instant. Head down, feet up — for an instant on the first day;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 125","title":"","sa":["क्षणाच्च किञ्चिदधिकमभ्यसेत्तु दिनेदिने ।","वली च पलितं चैव षण्मासार्धान्न दृश्यते ।"],"iast":["kṣaṇācca kiñcidadhikamabhyasettu dinedine |","valī ca palitaṃ caiva ṣaṇmāsārdhānna dṛśyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"Each day by increments — one should practice. Wrinkles and gray hair are not seen for six months and a half.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 126","title":"","sa":["याममात्रं तु यो नित्यमभ्यसेत्स तु कालजित् ।","वज्रोलीमभ्यसेद्यस्तु स योगी सिद्धिभाजनम् ।"],"iast":["yāmamātraṃ tu yo nityamabhyasetsa tu kālajit |","vajrolīmabhyasedyastu sa yogī siddhibhājanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"One who daily practices for a yāma (3 hours) — he is a conqueror of time. He who practices vajrolī — that yogi is a receptacle of perfection;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 127","title":"","sa":["लभ्यते यदि तस्यैव योगसिद्धिः करे स्थिता ।","अतीतानागतं वेत्ति खेचरी च भवेद्ध्रुवम् ।"],"iast":["labhyate yadi tasyaiva yogasiddhiḥ kare sthitā |","atītānāgataṃ vetti khecarī ca bhaveddhruvam |"],"words":[],"lit":"If perfection of yoga is obtained — it is in his hand. He knows past and future; khecarī surely arises.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 128","title":"","sa":["अमरीं यः पिबेन्नित्यं नस्यं कुर्वन्दिने दिने ।","वज्रोलीमभ्यसेन्नित्यममरोलीति कथ्यते ।"],"iast":["amarīṃ yaḥ pibennityaṃ nasyaṃ kurvandine dine |","vajrolīmabhyasennityamamarolīti kathyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"He who daily drinks amarī (rasa, immortal liquid), doing nasya (snuff) every day; who daily practices vajrolī — is called amarolī.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 129","title":"","sa":["ततो भवेद्राजयोगो नान्तरा भवति ध्रुवम् ।","यदा तु राजयोगेन निष्पन्ना योगिभिः क्रिया ।"],"iast":["tato bhavedrājayogo nāntarā bhavati dhruvam |","yadā tu rājayogena niṣpannā yogibhiḥ kriyā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then arises rāja-yoga; no obstacle exists in between, surely. When rāja-yoga is accomplished by yogis as practice,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 130","title":"","sa":["तदा विवेकवैराग्यं जायते योगिनो ध्रुवम् ।","विष्णुर्नाम महायोगी महाभूतो महातपाः ।"],"iast":["tadā vivekavairāgyaṃ jāyate yogino dhruvam |","viṣṇurnāma mahāyogī mahābhūto mahātapāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then arises discrimination and dispassion in the yogi, surely. Viṣṇu, the great Yogī, the great being, the great tapas-doer,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 131","title":"","sa":["तत्त्वमार्गे यथा दीपो दृश्यते पुरुषोत्तमः ।","यः स्तनः पूर्वपीतस्तं निष्पीड्य मुदमश्नुते ।"],"iast":["tattvamārge yathā dīpo dṛśyate puruṣottamaḥ |","yaḥ stanaḥ pūrvapītastaṃ niṣpīḍya mudamaśnute |"],"words":[],"lit":"Shines on the path of truth like a lamp — the Supreme Person. The breast which was previously sucked — that one squeezes and obtains joy.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 132","title":"","sa":["यस्माज्जातो भगात्पूर्वं तस्मिन्नेव भगे रमन् ।","या माता सा पुनर्भार्या या भार्या मातरेव हि ।"],"iast":["yasmājjāto bhagātpūrvaṃ tasminneva bhage raman |","yā mātā sā punarbhāryā yā bhāryā mātareva hi |"],"words":[],"lit":"From whom one was born — in that very organ one delights again. She who was mother becomes wife; she who was wife becomes mother.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 133","title":"","sa":["यः पिता स पुनः पुत्रो यः पुत्रः स पुनः पिता ।","एवं संसारचक्रं कूपचक्रेण घटा इव ।"],"iast":["yaḥ pitā sa punaḥ putro yaḥ putraḥ sa punaḥ pitā |","evaṃ saṃsāracakraṃ kūpacakreṇa ghaṭā iva |"],"words":[],"lit":"He who was father becomes son; he who was son becomes father. Thus the wheel of saṃsāra — like the wheel and pot of a well-rope —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 134","title":"","sa":["भ्रमन्तो योनिजन्मानि श्रुत्वा लोकान्समश्नुते ।","त्रयो लोकास्त्रयो वेदास्तिस्रः सन्ध्यास्त्रयः स्वराः ।"],"iast":["bhramanto yonijanmāni śrutvā lokānsamaśnute |","trayo lokāstrayo vedāstisraḥ sandhyāstrayaḥ svarāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Wandering, beings undergo birth — hearing of which, one obtains [release from] the worlds. Three worlds, three Vedas, three twilights, three svaras (musical notes);","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 135","title":"","sa":["त्रयोऽग्नयश्च त्रिगुणाः स्थिताः सर्वे त्रयाक्षरे ।","त्रयाणामक्षराणां च योऽधीतेऽप्यर्धमक्षरम् ।"],"iast":["trayo'gnayaśca triguṇāḥ sthitāḥ sarve trayākṣare |","trayāṇāmakṣarāṇāṃ ca yo'dhīte'pyardhamakṣaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"Three fires, three guṇas — all are established in the three-syllabled [OM]. Of the three syllables — even half a syllable — he who studies [it],","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 136","title":"","sa":["तेन सर्वमिदं प्रोतं तत्सत्यं तत्परं पदम् ।","पुष्पमध्ये यथा गन्धः पयोमध्ये यथा घृतम् ।"],"iast":["tena sarvamidaṃ protaṃ tatsatyaṃ tatparaṃ padam |","puṣpamadhye yathā gandhaḥ payomadhye yathā ghṛtam |"],"words":[],"lit":"By him, all this is woven through; that is truth, that is the supreme state. As fragrance in a flower, as ghee in milk,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 137","title":"","sa":["तिलमध्ये यथा तैलं पाषाणेष्विव काञ्चनम् ।","हृदि स्थाने स्थितं पद्मं तस्य वक्त्रमधोमुखम् ।"],"iast":["tilamadhye yathā tailaṃ pāṣāṇeṣviva kāñcanam |","hṛdi sthāne sthitaṃ padmaṃ tasya vaktramadhomukham |"],"words":[],"lit":"As oil in sesame, as gold within stones — established in the heart is the lotus; its face is downward.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 138","title":"","sa":["ऊर्ध्वनालमधोबिन्दुस्तस्य मध्ये स्थितं मनः ।","अकारे रेचितं पद्ममुकारेणैव भिद्यते ।"],"iast":["ūrdhvanālamadhobindustasya madhye sthitaṃ manaḥ |","akāre recitaṃ padmamukāreṇaiva bhidyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"Its stalk is upward, the bindu is below; in the middle is established the mind. By the syllable A, the lotus is emptied; by the syllable U it is pierced;","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 139","title":"","sa":["मकारे लभते नादमर्धमात्रा तु निश्चला ।","शुद्धस्फटिकसङ्काशं निष्कलं पापनाशनम् ।"],"iast":["makāre labhate nādamardhamātrā tu niścalā |","śuddhasphaṭikasaṅkāśaṃ niṣkalaṃ pāpanāśanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"By the syllable M one obtains the sound (nāda); by the half-mātrā (ardha-mātrā) it becomes still. Pure-crystal-like, stainless, sin-destroying —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 140","title":"","sa":["लभते योगयुक्तात्मा पुरुषस्तत्परं पदम् ।","कूर्मः स्वपाणिपादादिशिरश्चात्मनि धारयेत् ।"],"iast":["labhate yogayuktātmā puruṣastatparaṃ padam |","kūrmaḥ svapāṇipādādiśiraścātmani dhārayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"The yoga-united Self obtains the supreme state, the Person. As the tortoise withdraws hands, feet, and head into himself,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 141","title":"","sa":["एवं द्वारेषु सर्वेषु वायुपूरितरेचितः ।","निषिद्धं तु नवद्वारे ऊर्ध्वं प्राङ्निश्वसंस्तथा ।"],"iast":["evaṃ dvāreṣu sarveṣu vāyupūritarecitaḥ |","niṣiddhaṃ tu navadvāre ūrdhvaṃ prāṅniśvasaṃstathā |"],"words":[],"lit":"So in all the doors (of the senses), filled and released with air. The nine doors blocked, breathing upward and forward —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"18. Verses 104–142 · 142","title":"","sa":["घटमध्ये यथा दीपो निवातं कुम्भकं विदुः ।","निषिद्धैर्नवभिर्द्वारैर्निर्जने निरुपद्रवे ।"],"iast":["ghaṭamadhye yathā dīpo nivātaṃ kumbhakaṃ viduḥ |","niṣiddhairnavabhirdvārairnirjane nirupadrave |"],"words":[],"lit":"Like a lamp inside a pot — the windless kumbhaka so is known. The nine doors blocked, in a place free of disturbance,","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"19. Colophon","title":"","sa":["निश्चितं त्वात्ममात्रेणावशिष्टं योगसेवयेत्युपनिषत् ॥","इति योगतत्त्वोपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["niścitaṃ tv ātma-mātreṇāvaśiṣṭaṃ yoga-sevayety upaniṣat ||","iti yoga-tattvopaniṣat samāptā ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"What remains — determined as the Self alone — is to be reached by the service of yoga. So says the Upaniṣad.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["\"Thus ends the Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad.\""]},{"ref":"20. Glossary","title":"","sa":[],"words":[["Yoga-tattva","योगतत्त्व","The principle / essence of yoga"],["Aṣṭāṅga","अष्टाङ्ग","Eight-limbed (yoga)"],["Mantra-yoga","मन्त्रयोग","Yoga through repetition of mantra"],["Laya-yoga","लययोग","Yoga of dissolution (of citta)"],["Haṭha-yoga","हठयोग","Yoga of force/physical practice"],["Rāja-yoga","राजयोग","Royal yoga; the crowning practice"],["Ārambha","आरम्भ","Beginning (first stage of yoga)"],["Ghaṭa","घट","Vessel (second stage)"],["Paricaya","परिचय","Acquaintance (third stage)"],["Niṣpatti","निष्पत्ति","Completion (fourth stage)"],["Pūraka","पूरक","Inhalation"],["Kumbhaka","कुम्भक","Retention (of breath)"],["Recaka","रेचक","Exhalation"],["Kevala-kumbhaka","केवलकुम्भक","Pure / spontaneous retention (without inhale/exhale)"],["Iḍā","इडा","Left nāḍī"],["Piṅgalā","पिङ्गला","Right nāḍī"],["Suṣumnā","सुषुम्ना","Central nāḍī"],["Mātrā","मात्रा","Time-measure (~1 second)"],["Nāḍī-śuddhi","नाडीशुद्धि","Purification of channels"],["Pratyāhāra","प्रत्याहार","Sense-withdrawal"],["Dhāraṇā","धारणा","Concentration"],["Dhyāna","ध्यान","Meditation"],["Samādhi","समाधि","Absorption / equanimity of jīva and Paramātmā"],["Pañca-dhāraṇā","पञ्चधारणा","The five element-concentrations"],["Khecarī","खेचरी","Sky-walking mudrā (tongue back)"],["Mūla-bandha","मूलबन्ध","Root-lock"],["Jālandhara-bandha","जालन्धरबन्ध","Throat-lock"],["Uḍḍiyāna-bandha","उड्डियानबन्ध","Abdominal lock"],["Mahāmudrā","महामुद्रा","Great seal"],["Vajrolī","वज्रोली","A bindu-mastery practice"],["Kuṇḍalī","कुण्डली","Coiled (energy) at the base of the spine"],["Aṇimā etc.","अणिमादि","The eight major siddhis (powers)"],["Khecaratva","खेचरत्व","Sky-walking / movement through ether"],["Jīvanmukta","जीवन्मुक्त","Liberated while embodied"],["Hṛṣīkeśa","हृषीकेश","Viṣṇu — \"Lord of the senses\""],["Pitāmaha","पितामह","Brahmā — \"the Grandfather\""]],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"21. Editions and Sources","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Sanskrit text used in this document:","Source: Sanskrit Documents Project (sanskritdocuments.org)","File: yogatatva.itx — encoded and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi (Feb 18, 2000)","Description: 41/108 of the Muktikā Upaniṣads; Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda; Yoga Upaniṣad","Major published editions:","T.R. Srinivasa Ayyangar, The Yoga Upaniṣads (Adyar Library, 1938; revised 1952 by K. Krishna Warrier) — Sanskrit text + English translation of all 20 Yoga Upaniṣads","K. Narayanasvami Aiyar, Thirty Minor Upaniṣads (1914) — English translation of YT among others","A.G. Krishna Warrier, The Yoga Upaniṣads (Adyar Library, 1952) — the standard scholarly English edition","Sastri, S.S. Suryanarayana — translations published in Vedānta Press series","Online open-access resources:","sanskritdocuments.org — Sanskrit Devanāgarī and IAST text, freely downloadable","archive.org — multiple scanned editions, including the 1938 Adyar Library volume","classicyoga.co.in — verse-by-verse English translation with commentary","Sri Aurobindo Ashram — Hymns to the Mystic Fire and related materials reference the Yoga Upaniṣads"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"22. Closing","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Yoga Tattva Upaniṣad is one of the most practically-detailed Yoga Upaniṣads — a compact and complete manual of classical yoga as it was understood before the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā codified the later tradition. Its 142 verses cover everything from the philosophical foundation (yoga + jñāna both required), through the four yogas and the four stages, the eight limbs, the prāṇāyāma protocol, the seven mahā-mudrās, the five element-concentrations, the warning against siddhis, and the culminating definition of samādhi as the equality of jīvātmā and paramātmā.","Three principles to carry forward:","Knowledge and yoga are inseparable (v. 15). The intellectualist mistakes yoga for a body-practice; the practitioner mistakes knowledge for mere reading. The Yoga Tattva demands both.","Siddhis are obstacles (v. 76). The clearest classical warning against the spiritual ambition that mistakes powers for the goal.","Practice over talk (v. 80). \"By useless conversation nothing succeeds.\" The Upaniṣad pre-empts every scholastic tendency.","\"yogasevayetyupaniṣat\" — \"[Liberation] is reached by the service of yoga. Thus says the Upaniṣad.\" — Yoga Tattva colophon","🪔"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
